WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:18.450
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: – sitting to my right – ought
to be paired with a David.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.260
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Absolutely. We’re common
as muck.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.150
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Lord Prior is – well, you
are; he isn’t.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.220
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: That’s probably true, actually.
I’ll certainly agree with you on the – for

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.500
me, absolutely. I’m quite happy to be called
that.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.480
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: So, Lord Prior is unable to
be with us today, so I’m going to physically

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.280
chair the meeting, but David in his capacity
as the Deputy Chair of NHS England is chairing

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.210
the NHS England part of our committee in common,
if that makes – or board meeting in common,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.210
sorry.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Indeed.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.379
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay, so thank you for doing
that.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:52.400
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Good. I’m hoping not to
do very much. It’s in your tender mercies.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.220
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Well, should you be needed,
you’re here. The other apologies we’ve

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.659
had are Andrew Morris, David Behan, and, just
at the last minute, Lord Darzi has had to

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:08.159
go to a clinic to see some patients this afternoon.
And we should also acknowledge this being

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:12.730
Amanda Pritchard’s first meeting, so very
much welcome Amanda as the Chief Operating

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:17.250
Officer and Chief Executive of NHS Improvement,
so welcome to the team.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.800
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you very much.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Have we got any declarations

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:29.330
of interest that need to be added to the register?
Everyone happy? Okay. I can hear – there’s

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.640
an interesting television programme going
on in the background.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.940
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: I think it’s you.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Is it me? That’s even more

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:43.180
disturbing. Are there any comments on the
minutes and matters arising from the last

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:49.870
meeting? Any issues there, we should just
pull out? Jessica, anything on the matters

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.910
arising that we need to bring up?
&gt;&gt;Jessica Dahlstrom: No, it’s all in hand.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:57.120
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay, excellent. I know there
was one that was a meeting that – for Noel

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.300
and I, with NHS Resolution, that is in the
diary, if that’s not clear. Excellent.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:09.591
In which case, we’ll just quickly move on
to my update. Couple of things that I wanted

00:02:09.591 --> 00:02:15.180
to reference. The first thing I’d like to
do is to thank Jeremy Hughes and the Alzheimer’s

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:22.609
Society, who have just run a fantastic Dementia
Friends training session for the non-execs,

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:29.049
which has been a really brilliant break between
board meetings to help us learn, bring us

00:02:29.049 --> 00:02:33.689
back to really why we do this. You see a number
of us wearing our badges – but also, just

00:02:33.689 --> 00:02:39.060
helping us really raise our awareness of what
it feels like to have dementia and how all

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:45.450
of us can play a role in making our environments
both in the NHS and at home more dementia

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.459
friendly. So, thank you to Jeremy and his
team; we are now all Dementia Friends, which

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:54.969
is excellent. So, that’s the first thing.
Second thing: since our last board meeting,

00:02:54.969 --> 00:03:01.810
as ever, I have been out and about a fair
bit. So, for example, I spent an absolutely

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:11.430
eye-opening morning shadowing Dr Clare Gerada
in her clinic, which treats doctors, provides

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:18.120
mental-health support for doctors, which was
a great combination for me of getting closer

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.609
to our mental-health clinicians, but also
with our people plan agenda on what we need

00:03:22.609 --> 00:03:27.290
to do to support our clinicians working in
the NHS. What Clare and her team in Vauxhall

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:32.169
and across the country are doing is just that,
so that was hugely helpful for me. But I’ve

00:03:32.169 --> 00:03:37.999
also been out and about looking at AI at Moorfields,
at what Milton Keynes are doing in terms of

00:03:37.999 --> 00:03:45.529
engagement and becoming a great place to work.
Been to East Surrey with the trusts that are

00:03:45.529 --> 00:03:50.920
working with Virginia Mason on continuous
improvement. And to Bolton two weeks ago to

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.859
open their urology unit. So, I’ve been trying
to get out and about as much as usual. I used

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:58.409
to write that down, but I like people to know
where I’ve been going. And tomorrow, a number

00:03:58.409 --> 00:04:04.159
of us are off to Worthing to spend some time
with Marianne Griffiths and her team understanding

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:08.590
the patient-first approach to improvement
that they have in Worthing and in Brighton.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:12.900
So, those are my travels.
And then, the final thing I wanted to just

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:21.459
put on the record: we will be writing out
today to all provider chairs and lead governors

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:27.480
of foundation trusts with some work that’s
been underway for the last 18 months setting

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:35.140
out a development framework for NHS chairs,
an appraisal framework that underpins that

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:40.090
development and competency framework, and
a remuneration framework for chairs and non-executive

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:45.520
directors. It was – came to our people committee
quite a while ago and has been working its

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.620
way through the various levels of approval
that remuneration does require, and so that

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.740
will be heading out to the service writ large
this afternoon.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:05:02.420
So, that’s my update. Any questions, comments?
Excellent. In which case, we will hand over

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.540
to Simon.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: So, I think most of the substantial

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:09.780
items we need to talk about are elsewhere
on the agenda. Let me just mention five things

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:15.250
briefly, before we get into that. The first
is that, as we previously discussed, the single

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.590
biggest thing the Health Service has got to
get right, right now, is better support for

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:29.150
our frontline staff. We will next week therefore
be allocating the £150 million of professional

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:34.090
development funding which, since our last
meeting, Ruth and colleagues, we’ve been

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:40.230
able to secure, which will mean that our frontline
nurses and other health professionals will

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:47.030
have that earmarked training and development
support for the next three years and beyond.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:53.070
There’s obviously a lot of other work going
on in terms of how to expand training pipelines

00:05:53.070 --> 00:06:00.710
and better support for retention of staff
currently at work, but that’s a concrete

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:04.610
set of progress that’s been made since our
last meeting.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:11.110
Secondly, we have long argued that, despite
the fact that we’ve got a five-year revenue

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:17.430
funding settlement, the fact is that too much
of our buildings and equipment is out of date,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:24.220
and we have bottlenecks for diagnostics as
a result of that lack of capital investment.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:32.920
Since we last met, we have seen the improvements
in the availability of capital funding for

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:41.090
the current year, £1 billion extra of permission
to fund capital investment across the NHS,

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:47.340
and the beginnings of a hospital replacement
programme, with the first set of schemes announced.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:53.720
And we are hopeful that that is just the beginning
of what will be a bigger process of hospital

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:59.960
upgrades, but also mental-health services,
community health services, and primary care,

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.480
as well.
Thirdly, if you talk to anybody across the

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.180
frontline of the NHS right now, they would
say that the NHS is under real operational

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:16.940
pressure. Somebody put it in terms of, the
NHS feels full at the moment. And so part

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:21.820
of what we’re going to be discussing this
afternoon is the work underway not only to

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:26.170
try and deal with that in the here and now,
but the broader service redesign agenda to

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:30.850
relieve some of those pressures in the different
parts of the system, of which the work with

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:35.940
GPs, the work on same-day emergency care,
many other things, are all of a part. But

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:42.420
frankly, that is the key focus of leaders
across the NHS right now.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:50.400
Fourthly, that is in the context then of the
run-up to October 31st, and the exit from

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.790
the European Union. And so you’re going
to have a direct briefing from Keith Willett

00:07:55.790 --> 00:08:01.380
this afternoon around the NHS’s preparations,
in conjunction with the Department of Health

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.980
and Social Care, and other parts of government.
Fifthly, all of this is happening at a time

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:12.040
when we are now in the final stages over the
next two months or so, in the bringing together

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:16.790
of NHS England and NHS Improvement, and we
have to record the fact that that means it’s

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:22.420
a time of quite considerable uncertainty for
many hundreds of our staff across both organisations,

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:26.920
having had a big consultation and discussion
over the summer as to the shape that that

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:32.729
should look like. And so I just want to place
on record I think my thanks and our thanks

00:08:32.729 --> 00:08:37.250
as an entire board for the people who are
continuing to work so diligently on those

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:41.370
first four items, while nevertheless personally
experiencing the fifth.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:45.279
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yeah, absolutely. Excellent.
Any questions? Ruth?

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:49.870
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: It was more of a comment rather
than a question. So, Dido, you and I were

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:56.390
at the Queen’s Nursing Institute one day
this week, and both of us were given speeches.

00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:01.130
And yesterday, I had the opportunity to speak
with a number of DONs from across England,

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:06.750
Directors of Nursing from across England.
And the overwhelming positive feeling about

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:14.210
the CPD announcement of nurses receiving investment
in their continuing professional development,

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:21.140
not only is it appreciated about their own
career progression, about more likely to stay

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:26.899
within the NHS, but also for patient safety,
so they’d be safer practitioners. So, it’s

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:34.340
a great downpayment, and I know people are
very much appreciative of that.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:35.610
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: No, I agree. David?
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Simon, your comments about

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:41.210
the NHS feeling full I think resonates with
the – all the conversations that we’re

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:48.270
having. I think when we come into the section
later on, on the performance report and so

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:53.940
on, it would be really helpful to know what
we’re going to try to do to change that,

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:57.920
given that we are running into winter, in
particular, with whatever pressures that will

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.370
bring.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Absolutely. And both Amanda

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:05.950
and Pauline, I think, will use the time to
get into that. There are some things that

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:11.440
are in the gift of the NHS locally, there
are some things we’ve got to get right nationally,

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.310
there are some wider questions that frankly
are still not properly resolved, of which

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:23.170
the single biggest item is the pensions effect
on the availability of A&amp;E doctors, surgeons,

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:30.300
anaesthetists, and that does need a definitive
resolution, because what the Royal Colleges,

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:35.400
what clinical directors locally, individual
doctors, our trust leaders are telling us,

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:40.370
is that that is having a material effect on
the availability of staff to look after our

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.960
patients.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And you’re confident that

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.200
that’s understood in the appropriate parts
of government?

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.970
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think it’s understood
that there is an issue, and now we need to

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:56.140
move that understanding to a set of conclusions
that would resolve the matter.

00:10:56.140 --> 00:11:02.170
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay. Any other comments?

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:10.040
Okay. In which case, we should move onto our
next item, which is EU-exit readiness. Keith,

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:19.589
can you come and join us?
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: Thank you. Thank you very

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:25.779
much. My role in this is, I was appointed
as the Strategic Commander for EU Exit for

00:11:25.779 --> 00:11:35.020
the NHS, and with responsibility for taking
the NHS and its structures out of the European

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:40.480
Union, if indeed, that is the case, on 31st
October. And I would just remind everybody

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:46.440
at this point in time that the legal default
is that we – the UK does leave the European

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:55.380
Union on 31st October.
So, just to put things into context for everybody,

00:11:55.380 --> 00:12:00.560
the Department of Health and Social Care does
lead the response on EU exit, and is responsible

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:06.790
for the planning and the preparation of the
health and care sector, so NHS England and

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:14.570
NHS Improvement’s roles are to support that
process, to prepare and plan for any issues

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:19.670
or adverse incidents that may arise as a result,
and take the opportunity for benefits that

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:26.800
could also accrue by the work that we’re
doing. I’m pleased to report that the NHS

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:32.660
is in a good position of readiness at this
point in time, but we must recognise that

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:40.860
the potential scale and scope of a no-deal
exit means that there undoubtedly will be

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:47.529
some issues, and that there are elements that
are outside the control of NHS England or

00:12:47.529 --> 00:12:52.670
NHS Improvement, and that we are dependent
on government action in departments, and also

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:58.350
on industry. There is a paper that’s laid
before you which lays out the relationship

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:03.470
with the Department of Health and Social Care,
and the Government, and it also explains the

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:09.430
operational response structure that we have
put in place, which includes a national coordination

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:16.150
centre, regional coordination centre, a commercial
and procurement cell, as well as structures

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:23.870
within every NHS organisation to support an
EU exit. And that will – and they have been

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:30.310
given advice and support to make ready. The
make-ready process has been very individual.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:37.240
We have gone round the country and helped
the 400 NHS organisations, so that includes

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:41.570
the clinical commissioning groups, the acute
trusts, the ambulance services, mental-health

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:47.550
trusts, community trusts, and as well as the
key stakeholders, to help them understand

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:53.610
what a no-deal exit impact means to them in
terms of their workforce, the supply of medicines,

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:59.040
medical devices, what it means around data
and information flows, what it means around

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:03.649
the patients who are EU nationals living in
the UK, and indeed, UK nationals living overseas.

00:14:03.649 --> 00:14:10.160
They’ve had – so, an extensive exchange
has gone on, and they – those organisations

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:16.120
have been tasked to go back and to make the
changes and readiness within themselves in

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:20.940
anticipation of a no-deal exit.
The particular issues that I raise in the

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:27.110
paper are around, obviously, we have taken
the NHS through this in the end of March and

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:31.390
in mid-April, but this time, there are some
differences, is that we are going into winter

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:35.270
rather than coming out of winter, and it’s
already been alluded to that there are – will

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:40.529
be significant pressures within the system,
and we have aligned our winter operational

00:14:40.529 --> 00:14:46.560
response and our EU exit response, and ensured
that one isn’t surging into the other, as

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:52.550
it were, so that we are capable of managing
a heightened level of demand.

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:57.780
The other issue I have highlighted in the
paper is around our reliance on social care,

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:04.540
a sector we know that is actually very fragile
in many ways, has had more cuts over recent

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:10.089
years, and also, has a different employment
and provider model, which makes it more difficult

00:15:10.089 --> 00:15:15.910
to have perhaps the same national grasp that
we have in the NHS, and how we have encouraged

00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:21.529
the local health and care economies to come
together so that the NHS, indeed, can support

00:15:21.529 --> 00:15:26.140
social care where that will be of benefit,
particularly if they start to get into difficulty.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:31.240
And the third aspect I’ve raised is that,
clearly, at the moment, we don’t know what

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:35.649
the outcome and impact will be of a no-deal
exit. We have the government planning assumptions

00:15:35.649 --> 00:15:40.860
and we have worked to those, but there could
be issues going on for a lengthy period of

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:46.310
time, and we will need to address the resilience
within NHS England and NHS Improvement to

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:49.930
manage what could be a lengthy period of multiple
issues.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:56.110
So, that’s perhaps a brief update, and I’m
very happy, of course, to take any questions.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:16:03.459
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any comments or questions?
Thank you, Michelle.

00:16:03.459 --> 00:16:07.709
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Thank you. We had the
pleasure, really, of hearing the full briefing

00:16:07.709 --> 00:16:12.220
in the audit committee. I mean, is it possible
just to expand on some of the risks around

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:17.200
the social care workforce and what you think
they may need for the NHS? And perhaps describe

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:24.029
to us whether you have the team in place and
the resources to fulfil this programme.

00:16:24.029 --> 00:16:29.589
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: So, on the social care workforce,
yes, absolutely, and that would be one of

00:16:29.589 --> 00:16:35.580
our indirect risks, certainly in terms of
the NHS. So, the NHS workforce, about 6.5%

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:41.550
of our workforce are EU nationals, and they
will all be working under the terms and conditions

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:47.290
of the NHS, particularly agenda for change,
which covers care staff and nursing staff.

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:52.631
In the social care sector, it’s about 8%
are EU nationals, but like the NHS, there

00:16:52.631 --> 00:16:57.860
is a significant variation across the country.
So, in London and the South East in particular,

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:02.450
there are very high numbers of EU nationals
working in social care. Many of them will

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:08.189
be on an unstructured pay scale, and many
on relatively low pay scales within that.

00:17:08.189 --> 00:17:15.000
Clearly, the NHS is very dependent on social
care. We have about 100,000 beds in the NHS.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:20.790
In the care home sector alone, there are 450,000
beds, and about 1 million in total looked

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:26.930
after in the community. So, any impact on
the social care workforce, to either domiciliary

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:31.870
care or to the care home provision, would
have significant knock-on effects, hence why

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:38.810
I think we have been asking the NHS at the
health and local – and the social care community

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:43.290
locally, particularly through the local resilience
fora, to focus on this, to understand each’s

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:47.250
business continuity plans, to a level that
we haven’t done before.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:53.429
The other issue I think in the social care
workforce is, the provider sector is very

00:17:53.429 --> 00:17:59.900
fragmented; something like 85% of the care
home provision are small or medium enterprises,

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:04.820
they’re not large corporate organisations.
And therefore, we don’t have either the

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:10.480
same level of information about how prepared
they are, or, indeed, what their plans are

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:15.630
for resilience. Now, the Department of Health
and Social Care has that responsibility and

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:21.360
are working down through the directors of
adult social care and MCHLG to get assurance

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:27.770
in that sector, but clearly, it is an area
that remains vulnerable. And within government

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:33.559
planning assumptions, which are public, there
is a risk of the – of sterling value changing,

00:18:33.559 --> 00:18:37.970
and clearly, that would have potentially another
significant impact. I don’t see the social

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:44.220
care workforce issue as being a day-one issue,
but it could certainly become an issue over

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:48.660
time.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you. Any other questions?

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:58.970
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: What help do you need?
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: So, I think for me, the greatest

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:05.770
help I could have in the lead-up to 31st October
is to ensure that events like this, we do

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:11.010
get the information out to the public, to
the NHS staff and to our patients, just how

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:17.010
much preparation has gone on. And it is extensive
across health, in particular, and they should

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:23.130
be reassured that that has gone on. Also,
we have put in extensive response models in

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:28.120
order to deal with any incidents or issues
that do arise. Now, that confidence needs

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:33.430
to get to the public and to the patients,
because what would really hurt the NHS, and

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:38.170
therefore hurt patients, would be if behaviour
started to change unreasonably leading up

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:43.100
to that, so people started to do things differently,
ask for more medicines, that sort of thing,

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:47.950
which would clearly make – create difficulties
within the system which need not be there,

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:51.910
because all of that has been managed well
upstream. And we’re very used to it; the

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:56.430
NHS and our staff in the NHS in particular
are used to managing risk day in, day out.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.960
That’s what we do, whether you’re the
bed manager in a hospital allocating patients,

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:04.460
whether you’re the nurse in A&amp;E or the GP
in the surgery, or the paramedic at an accident

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:09.170
scene. Managing risk and prioritising is what
we do, so I think the public and our patients

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:12.530
can trust that we will do that through whatever
is presented to us.

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:16.270
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you, Keith. Steve?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Keith, I was just going to

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:21.110
expand on that point, because as you just
alluded to, the NHS, both nationally/regionally

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:28.850
and frontline staff are very used to dealing
with issues that arise, often with short notice,

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:36.860
where we need to, of course, correct, or we
need to put in support for supply chain disruption,

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:42.679
etc. So, I wondered if you could expand a
little bit on the sense of where the planning

00:20:42.679 --> 00:20:50.020
is building on expertise that we already have,
and where there may be some new risks, what

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:54.570
you have done to mitigate those new risks.
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: So – thank you. So, I’ll

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:58.770
use medicines as the example, because that’s
probably easier. So, there are a shortage

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:03.750
of medicines all the time, and have been for
years. At any one time, there are dozens if

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:09.330
not up to 100 medicines that the NHS will
be aware that are in – have some shortage.

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:14.440
That’s business of usual for us. Most of
those are managed well upstream with the suppliers,

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:19.309
with the manufacturers, and very rarely do
they come to – even to the notice of the

00:21:19.309 --> 00:21:24.170
public or patients. So, that system is all
in place. We’ve had that established across

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:31.169
government and across the NHS for many years.
What we’ve done is, throughout the EU exit

00:21:31.169 --> 00:21:36.730
planning, we have enhanced all the elements
in the normal pathways that we use. So, we’re

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:40.750
not asking people to do anything different
within the organisations; we’re following

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:45.230
the business-as-usual functions, how they
identify problems, how they escalate problems

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:49.670
to make us aware nationally, and then we’ve
increased the capacity to manage those nationally.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:53.650
So, that’s why I’m saying we’re confidently
well prepared in that regard.

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:58.480
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Wol?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thank you. Could you say a bit

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:01.130
more about how you’re working with the other
departments within government? Because of

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:04.330
course, it’s not working in isolation; you
have to interface with other people.

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:08.070
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: No, I’m pleased to say
that, having taken on this role, one of the

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:12.800
first things was that the Department of Health
and Social Care very much adopted the NHS

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:17.820
into their structures. So, we are represented
on all their boards, their assurance boards,

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:23.810
their supply boards, and their operational
boards, working very closely. They obviously

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:30.230
have the direct access to government departments,
but I have been confident that they have given

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:35.890
me all the information I need for the NHS
to make the necessary responses, and indeed,

00:22:35.890 --> 00:22:39.640
where appropriate, I’ve actually joined
them to meet with other departments, particularly

00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:44.250
perhaps, the Department for Transport, which
are key in terms of the freight capacity and

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:50.200
the supply chain to the NHS. And indeed, I’m
working with the Cabinet Office, as well.

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:53.040
So, in that regard, we’ve had a very good
relationship.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:57.500
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Keith, is there anything else

00:22:57.500 --> 00:23:06.500
that you need from us? Mindful particularly,
as Simon said earlier, the degree of additional

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:11.280
workload that people are feeling within NHS
England and NHS Improvement as we bring our

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:16.870
two organisations together, that extra stress.
Is there anything you need from us as two

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:19.330
combined boards to help you?
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: So, I think that is keeping

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:24.530
this very much on the agenda and recognising
the pressures in the system at all levels

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:30.440
in the NHS: across NHS England and Improvement
staff, but also very much down to the frontline

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:34.720
staff and across the community, and including
social care. I think we need to keep that

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:41.470
very high on our agenda, monitor it very carefully
and be prepared to respond as and when issues

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:46.250
arise. I think it – we’re always good
for managing incidents and problems over a

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:52.050
period of a few weeks and into a few months,
but there is a risk of exhaustion there, and

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:56.270
I think that will coincide with the winter,
and therefore, we will need to be particularly

00:23:56.270 --> 00:24:00.820
sensitive to that risk.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yeah. Wise words, wise words.

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:07.059
Any further questions or comments? In which
case, can we just formally thank you and your

00:24:07.059 --> 00:24:08.809
team, and your growing body of full-time and
reservists who are working on this? You give

00:24:08.809 --> 00:24:09.809
us great confidence with the professionalism
with which you’re leading this.

00:24:09.809 --> 00:24:10.809
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you.

00:24:10.809 --> 00:24:11.809
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Excuse me, Chair?
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I’m terribly sorry, but

00:24:11.809 --> 00:24:12.809
we don’t take questions in the middle of
the meeting.

00:24:12.809 --> 00:24:13.809
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I’ve had an email to say that
I’ve been asked an informal question before

00:24:13.809 --> 00:24:14.809
the meeting started.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I’m afraid the meeting has

00:24:14.809 --> 00:24:15.809
started. I will come and see you as soon as
the meeting finishes. If we could proceed,

00:24:15.809 --> 00:24:16.809
please.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: What time does the meeting finish?

00:24:16.809 --> 00:24:17.809
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: When we get to the end of
the agenda.

00:24:17.809 --> 00:24:18.809
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Right, I’m –
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I’m really sorry, but we

00:24:18.809 --> 00:24:19.809
do need –
[BREAK IN AUDIO]

00:24:19.809 --> 00:24:20.809
&gt;&gt;Speaker: – not having had any response
whatsoever. We’ve come in person. We merely

00:24:20.809 --> 00:24:21.809
want to hand you a copy of the email that
we sent you in July, so that we know that

00:24:21.809 --> 00:24:22.809
you’ve all seen it. And that is really it.
I mean, it’s just –

00:24:22.809 --> 00:24:23.809
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: So, I’m happy to take it
–

00:24:23.809 --> 00:24:24.809
[BREAK IN AUDIO]
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Why did I receive –

00:24:24.809 --> 00:24:25.809
[BREAK IN AUDIO]
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Send the email to me. We’ll

00:24:25.809 --> 00:24:26.809
review it, but we –
[BREAK IN AUDIO]

00:24:26.809 --> 00:24:27.809
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: – people who – sorry –
[BREAK IN AUDIO]

00:24:27.809 --> 00:24:28.809
&gt;&gt;Speaker: – asked me to just – I was
told at the beginning of the meeting I –

00:24:28.809 --> 00:24:29.809
[BREAK IN AUDIO]
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Could we move on to Digital

00:24:29.809 --> 00:24:30.809
First? Oh, sorry, I’ve stepped – missed
one. To legislation. Ian?

00:24:30.809 --> 00:24:31.809
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, Chair. In
February, we published our draft proposals.

00:24:31.809 --> 00:24:32.809
We struck a chord, generating 192,806 written
responses. In parallel, the Health and Social

00:24:32.809 --> 00:24:38.500
Care Select Committee conducted an enquiry.
In June, it published its conclusions and

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:44.880
recommendations welcoming our approach, albeit
with important caveats. Since then, my team

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:51.500
and I have worked intensively with many organisations
to shape our final proposals and reflect the

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:56.510
advice of the Select Committee. And today,
I’m pleased to report that we have achieved

00:24:56.510 --> 00:25:06.179
a clear consensus. This morning, a broad coalition
wrote to the Secretary of State, and I quote,

00:25:06.179 --> 00:25:13.660
‘With unanimous support for a focused NHS
bill within the forthcoming Queen’s Speech’,

00:25:13.660 --> 00:25:18.300
and they welcomed our recommendations and
encouraged the government to continue the

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:26.880
process of co-production. And the organisations
are the NHS Confederation, NHS Providers,

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:33.670
NHS Employers, NHS Clinical Commissioners,
the Community Provider Network, the King’s

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:39.429
Fund, the Royal College of Nursing, the Academy
of Medical Royal Colleges, the Royal College

00:25:39.429 --> 00:25:46.100
of General Practitioners, the Local Government
Association, the Patients Association, Healthwatch

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:53.080
England, the Richmond Group of 14 major health
charities, National Voices, Unison, ICS Leaders,

00:25:53.080 --> 00:26:03.320
and the NHS Assembly co-chairs.
Turning to our recommendations. We continue

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:12.179
to say: scrap Section 75 of the Health and
Social Care Act 2012. Remove the 2012 Act

00:26:12.179 --> 00:26:19.970
role of the Competition and Mergers Authority,
and remove the NHS from jurisdiction of the

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:28.990
Public Contract Regulations 2015. Our proposed
best value regime should be renamed, co-produced

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:35.320
with the NHS Assembly, and published in draft
alongside a bill. Patient choice should be

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:41.710
protected. The triple aim duty should include
wellbeing as well as health. It should include

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:47.730
a new duty to engage local communities. The
government should also explore establishing

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:54.350
the principle of community co-production within
the NHS constitution. We drop the proposal

00:26:54.350 --> 00:27:01.419
to force provider mergers. The proposal to
set capital controls for foundation trusts

00:27:01.419 --> 00:27:08.940
has been heavily circumscribed as a reserve
power only, with numerous safeguards. Only

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:17.429
NHS providers should be able to hold integrated
care provider contracts. Integrated care systems

00:27:17.429 --> 00:27:22.280
should be further supported and strengthened
by the ability to create joint committees

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:28.700
across multiple providers and commissioners,
and the transparency of their decision-making

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:35.770
improved. As the boards have previously discussed,
we recommend the full legal merger of NHS

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:41.320
England and NHS Improvement, which is, of
course, actually Monitor and the Trust Development

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:46.880
Authority. Given the three have different
accountability arrangements, that will have

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:52.679
to be reviewed and clarified in a bill. The
proposal to allow the Secretary of State to

00:27:52.679 --> 00:27:58.429
change arm’s-length-body functions should
be dropped. The government should revisit

00:27:58.429 --> 00:28:04.809
national accountabilities for workforce functions,
as advocated by the Royal College of Nursing

00:28:04.809 --> 00:28:11.309
and others. In order to make integrated care
a reality, other changes are needed that don’t

00:28:11.309 --> 00:28:15.480
need legislation, and there may be a case
for the department additionally to consider

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:20.380
whether a bill could help by providing a clear
and transparent underpinning for information

00:28:20.380 --> 00:28:24.700
sharing and IT interoperability within the
NHS.

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:34.770
So, we were asked by government to make recommendations.
We have now demonstrably channelled what the

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:40.480
NHS and its wider partners in local government
and the voluntary and community sector want

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:47.140
and do not want from NHS legislation, and
I commend these proposals to our boards, to

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:53.540
government and to Parliament.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Questions or comments?

00:28:53.540 --> 00:29:00.400
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Thank you, Chair. First of all,
I think it was a remarkable response, 192,000.

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:07.090
I think it does show the depth of commitment
to the service out there, and their wish to

00:29:07.090 --> 00:29:12.230
be involved, and the fact we’ve listened.
I just – on a specific, I just wonder if

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:18.740
you’d expand a bit upon the Select Committee’s
airless room, and what the thinking is there

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:26.651
about ensuring choice.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, on the Select Committee,

00:29:26.651 --> 00:29:31.140
we’re quoting David Hare, who represents
NHS – a particular set of providers. And

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:37.710
their view was that it’s very important
that, in the best interests of patients and

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:44.170
the public, the core principles of patient
choice which have been enshrined in the NHS

00:29:44.170 --> 00:29:49.929
since its inception in 1948 – very first
line, first choose your GP – actually are

00:29:49.929 --> 00:29:55.770
maintained. So, how do we make sure that that
happens alongside the increased collaboration?

00:29:55.770 --> 00:30:02.640
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Ruth?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, Chair. Thank

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:05.929
you, Ian, for all of the work you’ve done
on a wide range of these issues. But referring

00:30:05.929 --> 00:30:13.970
particularly to the recommendation number
26, I’m grateful and I support wholeheartedly

00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:20.720
the recommendation that the government does
now revisit with partners – RCN, Royal College

00:30:20.720 --> 00:30:28.600
of Midwives, Unison, and others, as well – how
we look at the responsibility nationally for

00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:33.870
duties in terms of workforce functions and
accountability, and having more clarity on

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:36.466
that, I certainly would welcome as Chief Nursing
Officer, so thank you.

00:30:36.466 --> 00:30:37.466
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Other questions or comments?
Simon, is there anything that you’d like

00:30:37.466 --> 00:30:50.390
to add?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, I mean, as Ian implies,

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:54.020
we are actually building on a big consensus
that has emerged across the NHS about some

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:59.010
of the changes that people would like to see;
rightly so, in order to accelerate our joined-up

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:03.340
care and the long-term plan implementation.
I don’t think anybody’s naïve about the

00:31:03.340 --> 00:31:08.040
particular parliamentary circumstances facing
the nation right now, so this is not just

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:13.309
a question for the immediate here and now;
this is actually, whenever that time comes

00:31:13.309 --> 00:31:18.790
for Parliament to address these matters, here
is a very solid and consensus-based set of

00:31:18.790 --> 00:31:26.800
practical proposals, and whether that time
is imminent or a little way off, these proposals

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:30.340
will be ready for that moment.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Wol?

00:31:30.340 --> 00:31:36.330
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Just one quick question. Ian,
are you able to estimate the benefit, the

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:42.679
cost saving perspective, of the duplication
that’s been present in the system, to the

00:31:42.679 --> 00:31:44.880
benefit of actually implementing this?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I don’t have a particular number

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:50.620
I can quote, but one of the things that the
NHS has been saying, particularly in relation

00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:56.730
to procurement, is that there’s an automatic
requirement now under the existing legislation,

00:31:56.730 --> 00:32:01.010
the combination of Section 75 and then the
Public Procurement Regulations, that when

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:07.169
you hit a trigger point, there has to be a
procurement for those services. And we hear

00:32:07.169 --> 00:32:13.570
not just from commissioners and NHS providers,
but also other providers, including in the

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:19.350
independent sector, that say, ‘Look, it’s
nuts to have to go through this procurement

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:23.289
process’. And what we’re trying to do
is to make sure that we can reduce avoidable

00:32:23.289 --> 00:32:30.179
bureaucracy associated with that. And what
the proposals overall are mainly doing is,

00:32:30.179 --> 00:32:35.740
they’re removing barriers, so restrictions
that make it hard for people to work with

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:40.340
each other, and that’s really the essence
of almost everything in this set of propositions.

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:45.830
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: So, on that basis, are both

00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:56.720
boards happy to approve these recommendations?
Excellent. Great piece of work. So, now we

00:32:56.720 --> 00:33:02.260
will move onto Digital First primary care.
If Dr Nikki Kanani and Ed Waller can join

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:05.240
us? I think there’s chairs for both of you,
just.

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:10.059
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: We’re used to getting cosy
on this side of the table, so thank you for

00:33:10.059 --> 00:33:17.250
having us, and we’re asking you to formally
agree the publication of our Digital First

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:21.730
primary care consultation, which you’ll
all have in your packs today. And a huge thank-you

00:33:21.730 --> 00:33:28.060
to the teams who pulled this together; it
was a truly heroic effort, and I hope that

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:32.150
it answers a lot of the questions that you
have been asking over the past few months.

00:33:32.150 --> 00:33:36.880
To set the scene: I’m Nikki, I’m a GP
in London and director of primary care here

00:33:36.880 --> 00:33:40.240
at NHS E&amp;I.
&gt;&gt;Ed Waller: And I’m Ed Waller; I’m the

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:43.450
director responsible for primary care strategy
in our NHS contracting machines.

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:48.120
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: And this is part, I think,
of a wider narrative. You’ve all got examples

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:52.740
of places round the country where general
practice particularly can offer really good

00:33:52.740 --> 00:33:57.480
digital services for their patients. And actually,
I think for decades, general practice have

00:33:57.480 --> 00:34:02.419
been digital pioneers, and we can see those
examples in some spaces round the country.

00:34:02.419 --> 00:34:07.400
And what we want to do is offer that to all
of our patients around the country. I still

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:11.889
struggle sometimes to log on in my own practice,
and I want to make sure that that isn’t

00:34:11.889 --> 00:34:17.470
the case for my colleagues. Because actually,
we’ve got far more to focus on. And actually,

00:34:17.470 --> 00:34:23.119
more so for our patients. Recently, I had
a patient tell me that as a nomadic boat-dweller,

00:34:23.119 --> 00:34:27.570
she’s unable to get the care that she really
needs. What that meant for her was that she

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:33.159
was diagnosed with breast cancer at stage
IV. We can avoid that, and what this paper

00:34:33.159 --> 00:34:37.470
does is begin to set out that story and those
first steps.

00:34:37.470 --> 00:34:40.649
This story actually started last year, when
we began to negotiate the GP contract, and

00:34:40.649 --> 00:34:46.280
when we came to – in January to set out
that we will offer Digital First care across

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:53.260
primary care by 2020/2021. And what that really
means is making sure that all our practices

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:57.690
can digitise their offer. So, actually, we
don’t talk about Digital First primary care,

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:03.849
but primary care offering all manners of ways
to access your appropriate primary care professional.

00:35:03.849 --> 00:35:07.260
Because that’s not always going to be the
GP, and in fact, over the next five years,

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:12.140
as we see more staff coming into general practice
and primary care, it could be and it will

00:35:12.140 --> 00:35:15.780
be the right person for the care needs that
you have.

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:20.070
So, we’ve set out a framework and we’ve
set out a timeframe, and this framework and

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:25.180
timeframe will need time and capacity to embed
in general practice, because it is a change

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:29.910
that will be taking our practices on, but
one that we must necessarily do. As we’ve

00:35:29.910 --> 00:35:33.000
gone through this journey, we’ve realised
that there are a number of key questions that

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:37.589
we need to answer in order to really get us
effectively on that journey, this being very

00:35:37.589 --> 00:35:41.170
much the beginning. And I’m going to ask
Ed to set out some of those key additional

00:35:41.170 --> 00:35:46.970
requirements that are in the pack, AND then
we can open up to questions.

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:51.200
&gt;&gt;Ed Waller: Thanks, Nikki. So, the consultation
itself focused on some of the changes we think

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:55.750
– thought were required to the commissioning
contracting machines to take account of the

00:35:55.750 --> 00:36:01.400
fact that new models of primary care were
expanding, and expanding in a way that wasn’t

00:36:01.400 --> 00:36:06.710
foreseen when the commissioning/contracting
arrangements were designed. So, effectively,

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:13.470
we had providers who were registering patients
as out-of-area patients, i.e., outside their

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:18.940
GP practices’ catchment, and providing to
them a Digital First offer: a Digital First

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:24.020
offer we think patients should have the choice
of, and as Nikki described, by 2021, all patients

00:36:24.020 --> 00:36:29.070
will have that type of service. But we need
to reconcile some of the way that is currently

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:34.500
working, with the rules. And we can break
that down into three categories.

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:40.750
So, on the first, we propose that where a
provider registers lots of patients out of

00:36:40.750 --> 00:36:45.839
their practice catchment area, there should
be a threshold at which that list is broken

00:36:45.839 --> 00:36:54.670
up to return patients to a practice list that
is – registers them in their own CCG and

00:36:54.670 --> 00:36:59.030
allows those patients to be delivered integrated
care with other providers. So, above 1,000

00:36:59.030 --> 00:37:05.210
patients, we’re going to take forward a
proposal to disaggregate large contracts that

00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:10.540
have registered out-of-area patients, and
new APMS lists will be established in the

00:37:10.540 --> 00:37:17.770
CCGs where more than 1,000 patients are residing,
and those new APMS providers will be required

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:23.440
to join local primary care networks.
In the first instance, that means a single

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:29.770
list operating in Hammersmith and Fulham will
turn into 17 lists, one in Hammersmith and

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:38.530
Fulham, and one in 16 other CCGs where more
than 1,000 patients are registered.

00:37:38.530 --> 00:37:42.970
There are than a set of financial questions.
One of them is the speed at which the financial

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:48.920
allocation process reconciles large-scale
movements of patients. We are taking forward

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:54.070
on the back of the consultation a mechanism
that would speed up the reconciliation process,

00:37:54.070 --> 00:38:01.670
so that between financial years, a quarterly
mechanism would reconcile the movement of

00:38:01.670 --> 00:38:07.780
large numbers of patients between CCGs. And
that will take account of the demography of

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:13.440
those patients, their age and their gender,
and it’ll take account of the practice’s

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:19.510
position from whence they came and the deprivation
of the original practice.

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:24.330
We also asked questions around the way in
which we fund practices, and whether Digital

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:31.420
First models should ask us – cause us to
ask questions about whether that was right.

00:38:31.420 --> 00:38:35.220
One of those questions was around whether
out-of-area patients – and these are patients

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:42.589
who register away from their home – should
be – should count – should have less value

00:38:42.589 --> 00:38:48.380
in the general practice funding formula than
patients who are in-area patients, and the

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:52.430
reason for that would be, the obligation on
the GP to provide home visits disappears.

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:59.150
We’re very clear that the proportionality
of rejigging the entire NHS primary care allocation

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:02.970
systems, trying to account for what are a
very small number of patients, most of whom

00:39:02.970 --> 00:39:06.910
don’t actually receive home visits because
of their demographics, would be totally disproportionate.

00:39:06.910 --> 00:39:13.431
We propose to leave that as it is. And we
also asked about whether the 46% that a practice

00:39:13.431 --> 00:39:18.770
received for a new patient to take account
of the extra work involved in registering

00:39:18.770 --> 00:39:22.940
a new patient, should be changed to take account
of digital models. We’ve heard very strong

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:28.119
feedback through the consultation that to
try and make changes to that mechanism also

00:39:28.119 --> 00:39:32.400
runs the risk of destabilising practices who
have a naturally high turnover, and again,

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:35.890
the proportionality of the number of people
we’re talking about on the Digital First

00:39:35.890 --> 00:39:42.760
models doesn’t warrant changing that system.
The final thing the consultation asked, and

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:48.090
which we’re now proposing to take forward,
is a mechanism by which under-doctored parts

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:54.210
of England would become places where new lists
could be established under a nationally run

00:39:54.210 --> 00:40:01.359
procurement regime. We would nationally certify
providers who were suitable to take up opportunities

00:40:01.359 --> 00:40:09.770
automatically in the places where – the
20% most under-doctored CCGs, and that builds

00:40:09.770 --> 00:40:15.291
on the fact that in the early evaluations
of the Digital First models, there is some

00:40:15.291 --> 00:40:20.110
emerging evidence that you can bring in GP
capacity through Digital First provision that

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:24.670
isn’t available if you try and force people
to work in a face-to-face model, and we want

00:40:24.670 --> 00:40:30.320
to make sure, if that is going to happen,
it happens in places that need doctors most.

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:38.010
In terms of next steps, the requirement to
disaggregate lists that are large and based

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:42.830
on out-of-area registration requires changes
to the law. It requires the Department to

00:40:42.830 --> 00:40:47.140
change the regulations governing the GP contract.
We will need to discuss the details of that

00:40:47.140 --> 00:40:51.490
with the BMA, and hope to implement it as
soon as changes can be made, which might well

00:40:51.490 --> 00:40:56.390
be as soon as April.
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: So, thank you. I hope this

00:40:56.390 --> 00:41:02.330
offers a balance between digital innovation
and how to futureproof ourselves, but continues

00:41:02.330 --> 00:41:06.430
to value continuity of area, which we – I
know we highly value within current models

00:41:06.430 --> 00:41:11.150
of general practice. Happy to take questions.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Munir?

00:41:11.150 --> 00:41:19.230
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: Thanks very much, Dido.
Can you explain how you came up with the number

00:41:19.230 --> 00:41:24.339
of 1,000 as your threshold?
&gt;&gt;Ed Waller: So, the original consultation

00:41:24.339 --> 00:41:31.890
proposed that the number should be between
1,000 and 2,000. We’re trying to balance

00:41:31.890 --> 00:41:36.420
two things, really. We’re trying to balance
the fact that we’re trying to return patients

00:41:36.420 --> 00:41:41.430
to a practice that is based locally and is
able to integrate the care it offers with

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:47.560
other local services, as soon as it is practical
to manage the implications of running a practice,

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:54.280
i.e., have enough patients to make running
a practice sustainable. We got a lot of consultation

00:41:54.280 --> 00:42:01.060
feedback about where that boundary was, and
settled on 1,000 as a sort of practical midpoint

00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:05.230
in some of the considerations we had to take
account of on that.

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:11.390
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay. Noel?
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Yeah, I think this is a very

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:17.141
positive step forward overall, and thank you
very much for pulling it all together. With

00:42:17.141 --> 00:42:24.229
respect to paragraph 12 on how you’re targeting
new opportunities on areas of greatest need

00:42:24.229 --> 00:42:31.480
– and some of those areas of greatest need
are in deprived areas – in NHS Digital,

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:37.250
we spend a lot of time testing digital products
on the spectrum of inequalities and the spectrum

00:42:37.250 --> 00:42:46.859
of needs, particularly in areas which are
not digitally adept. When you certify nationally

00:42:46.859 --> 00:42:52.570
some of the providers for Digital First services,
what kind of assurance mechanisms do you think

00:42:52.570 --> 00:42:58.260
you’ll have in place to ensure they adopt
the same standards of testing suitability

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:02.520
of digital products in deprived areas as we
do today?

00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:11.520
&gt;&gt;Ed Waller: So, the next phase of this work
is actually to design precisely what APMS

00:43:11.520 --> 00:43:16.589
contractual arrangement those new market entrants
would be offered, and the processes by which

00:43:16.589 --> 00:43:22.650
we would do – we’d make such assurances
as that. I think one of the important things

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:28.800
on accessibility is that we want to put a
requirement on any provider that enters the

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:35.020
market in one of these areas to make an effort
to register with their – in their provider

00:43:35.020 --> 00:43:42.030
model a cross section of the population. So,
this shouldn’t be about putting providers

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:47.460
into under-doctored parts of England and then
providing services preferentially to people

00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:51.540
who might well be, even in that CCG, receiving
better healthcare than others.

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:57.060
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Joanne?
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Like Noel, I think this is

00:43:57.060 --> 00:44:05.790
a really positive step forward, so thank you
for that. I wanted to just sort of ask about

00:44:05.790 --> 00:44:10.630
the broadening the lens of it. I think when
we talk about Digital First primary care,

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:15.720
we can sometimes get a bit overfocused on
video consultations between patients and professionals,

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:20.540
which I would sort of call remote doctoring.
It’s a great thing to have. But actually,

00:44:20.540 --> 00:44:27.000
if you ask patients in a more sort of market
researchy sort of way what do they want, you’ll

00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:32.079
hear a lot about, ‘I want to be able to
monitor my long-term condition using an app,

00:44:32.079 --> 00:44:36.450
I want to be able to ask clinicians a question,
I want to be able to sort out my appointments,

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:41.510
I want to be able to sort out my medicines,
I want to be able to see my record. So, I

00:44:41.510 --> 00:44:46.240
just wondered if you could tell us a bit more
about how you see the staff beyond the – if

00:44:46.240 --> 00:44:51.560
you like, the remote doctoring and the appointments;
how that might develop as a result of the

00:44:51.560 --> 00:44:54.230
proposals that you’re putting forward.
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Great, so I’ll kick off,

00:44:54.230 --> 00:44:58.250
if that’s all right. This is something we’ve
been considering really closely through the

00:44:58.250 --> 00:45:03.000
national review of access, which I’m currently
chairing. A big part of this is exactly what

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:08.520
you describe. What patients talk about is
much more about controlling and re-empowering

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:12.359
themselves with their own healthcare. So,
actually, what they want to be able to do

00:45:12.359 --> 00:45:18.760
is not phone 200 times to get hold of their
GP, and not have to almost fight to get their

00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:22.619
information uploaded or to pull down their
own information. So, I think this is just

00:45:22.619 --> 00:45:26.560
the beginning of setting out what the – sort
of what the basic offer is across general

00:45:26.560 --> 00:45:30.390
practice, but particularly through primary
care networks, we’ll be able to offer a

00:45:30.390 --> 00:45:37.480
much wider array of services. So, working
with colleagues in NHSX, we’re talking about

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:42.130
tech banks, so people can then get their iPads
to do their remote monitoring of their long-term

00:45:42.130 --> 00:45:46.859
condition, to make sure that apps are fully
interoperable and can sit behind the NHS apps

00:45:46.859 --> 00:45:51.830
so that they can – people can look after
their own healthcare. I mean, as I say, my

00:45:51.830 --> 00:45:55.940
children are seven and 11, and they’re not
going to sit in the GP surgery waiting for

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:59.910
pretty much any aspect of their healthcare.
We need to be really mindful of that as we

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:05.310
develop. So, I think the implications are
huge in terms of how we can support practices

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:09.690
to broaden their offer, but with the support
and resilience of other practices through

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:13.110
the network.
&gt;&gt;Ed Waller: I guess the only other thing

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:18.820
to add is that the GP contract deal that the
board saw in January also set out a series

00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:24.211
of milestones on the provision of services
in a digital manner. So, for example, this

00:46:24.211 --> 00:46:31.130
year will see 25% of practice appointments
available to book online, and the same sort

00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:35.329
of requirement is in place around online access
to your patient record, prescription ordering,

00:46:35.329 --> 00:46:40.490
etc. So, slowly, the whole system moves towards
a whole gamut of things being provided in

00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:44.660
a digital way.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: That’s going to be really

00:46:44.660 --> 00:46:50.240
essential in closing what will otherwise be
an ever-widening gap between the workforce

00:46:50.240 --> 00:46:55.420
that we have available and the – people’s
increasing health needs. So, that focus on

00:46:55.420 --> 00:47:00.500
using technology to improve people’s ability
to look after themselves and to, as you say,

00:47:00.500 --> 00:47:03.460
kind of take power and control back, is just
really encouraging. Thank you.

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:07.210
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: And just an aside, the – I
think it’s the second or third paragraph

00:47:07.210 --> 00:47:11.960
here, we talk about the change management
capacity that general practice needs, and

00:47:11.960 --> 00:47:15.730
primary care, and it’s almost, we need to
just set aside the text slightly, because

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:19.890
once we get that right and we get people feeling
more comfortable with their tech and, you

00:47:19.890 --> 00:47:25.359
know, the fax machines that definitely aren’t
in my surgery are moved away from, we will

00:47:25.359 --> 00:47:30.210
be on a different journey with developing
tech and embedding it in the way that you

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:32.220
describe.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thank you.

00:47:32.220 --> 00:47:41.740
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any further questions or comments?
In which case, are we happy to approve the

00:47:41.740 --> 00:47:48.119
recommendations? Well done. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Thanks.

00:47:48.119 --> 00:47:55.380
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: So, we move on now to the
clinically led review of NHS access standards.

00:47:55.380 --> 00:47:58.599
Steve, are you going to kick us off?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, thank you, Dido. So,

00:47:58.599 --> 00:48:06.339
as the board will recall, in June of last
year, the Prime Minister asked NHS England

00:48:06.339 --> 00:48:11.630
as part of the development of the long-term
plan to review the key access standards used

00:48:11.630 --> 00:48:20.300
within the NHS in England. And specifically,
the ask was to ensure that in developing a

00:48:20.300 --> 00:48:26.950
service plan for the next five to ten years,
we ensured that those access standards were

00:48:26.950 --> 00:48:33.750
up to date and were fit for purpose for the
service developments that we would be proposing.

00:48:33.750 --> 00:48:39.230
And I guess you can think about that in two
ways, and we approached it in two ways. Firstly,

00:48:39.230 --> 00:48:44.130
for some of these standards which have been
in place for up to 15 years, what have we

00:48:44.130 --> 00:48:47.740
learned, what are their strengths, what are
their weaknesses? In essence, how could we

00:48:47.740 --> 00:48:54.650
improve upon the current standards? And secondly,
as I have alluded to in terms of the service

00:48:54.650 --> 00:48:59.790
models that have developed during that period,
and in our work set out in the long-term plan

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:06.110
to develop them further, are those standards
supportive of the direction of travel that

00:49:06.110 --> 00:49:10.990
those services models are taking, i.e., do
they support the service and support patients

00:49:10.990 --> 00:49:18.650
in delivering the service models that we have
developed and we are proposing?

00:49:18.650 --> 00:49:25.440
So, the long-term plan was published in January,
and in that, we promised that we would publish

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:33.890
an interim report in the spring, and we did
so in March publish an interim report, which

00:49:33.890 --> 00:49:39.980
set out a proposed set of updated and improved
standards. I’m not going to go through those

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:45.380
in detail; they are summarised in the paper,
and of course, in more detail in the already-published

00:49:45.380 --> 00:49:50.230
interim report. But I will remind the board
that they’re in four key areas. The first

00:49:50.230 --> 00:49:56.380
is urgent and emergency care, where there
have been standards, the A&amp;E four-hour standard,

00:49:56.380 --> 00:50:05.190
for around 15 years. In elective care, where
key standard is around 18 weeks, standard

00:50:05.190 --> 00:50:13.800
for treatment from referral. In care, where
key standard is a 62-day standard from referral

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:19.000
to first treatment, and some two-week access
standards. And in mental health, and I think

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:24.170
particularly important in mental health; this
is an area where we have had very few access

00:50:24.170 --> 00:50:29.910
standards, and I think the importance of proposing
a set of standards in mental health that will

00:50:29.910 --> 00:50:36.030
bring mental health parity in terms of access
standards with physical health is extremely

00:50:36.030 --> 00:50:41.839
important.
So, as part of that report, we emphasise the

00:50:41.839 --> 00:50:50.180
importance of testing the proposed standards
to ensure that we learned how they might work

00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:55.530
in the real world rather than on – in a
report. And therefore, in the next phase of

00:50:55.530 --> 00:51:04.250
the work, we have moved into a testing process
with NHS organisations. We commenced the testing

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:09.850
in the early summer, so urgent and emergency
care went first at the end of May. And we

00:51:09.850 --> 00:51:14.900
have been rolling out our testing over the
last few months, and will continue to roll

00:51:14.900 --> 00:51:17.859
that.
By nature of these standards and by nature

00:51:17.859 --> 00:51:23.089
of the patient pathways and journeys that
they encompass, the testing periods will be

00:51:23.089 --> 00:51:28.630
different for each of the standards, and therefore,
the timelines are not unified into one single

00:51:28.630 --> 00:51:33.829
timeline. So, as I’ve said, urgent and emergency
care we rolled out first, and probably some

00:51:33.829 --> 00:51:39.510
of the mental-health standards we will be
rolling out towards the end of the testing

00:51:39.510 --> 00:51:44.930
period.
So, at this point, I should pause to say that

00:51:44.930 --> 00:51:49.860
I’m extremely grateful, as I think we all
are, to those NHS organisations that have

00:51:49.860 --> 00:51:56.099
joined with us and agreed to be test sites.
I don’t underestimate the work involved

00:51:56.099 --> 00:52:02.360
in doing that, and for the around a dozen
or so – depending on the testing area that

00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:08.790
we have used in each of these protocols, testing
protocols – I’m really grateful to the

00:52:08.790 --> 00:52:12.980
organisations, the boards, and indeed, the
front-line staff who have joined us on this

00:52:12.980 --> 00:52:21.580
journey to test a set of new, improved standards.
I’m not going to comment in detail on them.

00:52:21.580 --> 00:52:25.839
We may comment a bit more on urgent and emergency
care, because that has been going the longest,

00:52:25.839 --> 00:52:30.340
and I’m really grateful to Pauline Philip
and the team – Pauline is over there, who’s

00:52:30.340 --> 00:52:36.790
been leading this. And Pauline might want
to comment on some of the high-level learning

00:52:36.790 --> 00:52:41.680
from that initial phase. But I would say that
our experience has been that the sites that

00:52:41.680 --> 00:52:49.220
we have recruited have enjoyed working on
this. They want to be part of the journey

00:52:49.220 --> 00:52:54.740
of improving standards, and they have worked
very closely with us, and they have found

00:52:54.740 --> 00:53:01.819
it useful, interesting and helpful to be able
to revisit some of the aspects of their operational

00:53:01.819 --> 00:53:06.860
performance. So, in urgent and emergency care,
where we have suggested a standard that encompasses

00:53:06.860 --> 00:53:12.200
total time that a patient spends in an A&amp;E
department rather than focusing just on a

00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:17.150
four-hour period, has allowed organisations
to really think about every minute that a

00:53:17.150 --> 00:53:25.130
patient spends in an A&amp;E department. It’s
too early to discuss data in detail, but as

00:53:25.130 --> 00:53:29.600
I say, Pauline may want to give some more
high-level information.

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:34.829
The third point I would make in introducing
the paper is that we have always involved

00:53:34.829 --> 00:53:40.210
partners in the development of this, and since
March, we have expanded the stakeholders and

00:53:40.210 --> 00:53:45.660
partners that we have worked with. So, we
have, in addition to an oversight group that

00:53:45.660 --> 00:53:51.520
has looked at the work as a whole, we have
introduced specific expert groups in all of

00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:57.280
the four areas – in cancer and mental health,
using groups that we already have as part

00:53:57.280 --> 00:54:02.300
of the long-term plan – and that has been
invaluable in giving us particular expert

00:54:02.300 --> 00:54:07.060
advice. And again, I’m very grateful to
those organisations and those individuals

00:54:07.060 --> 00:54:10.660
who’ve worked with us on this, and that
includes the variety of professional groups

00:54:10.660 --> 00:54:16.941
such as colleges, both colleges – doctors
and colleges and nurses, and others, to patient

00:54:16.941 --> 00:54:23.550
groups and to charities. And their ongoing
help in this is really important.

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:28.370
And finally, I would say something about next
steps, which is, again, in the paper. So,

00:54:28.370 --> 00:54:33.280
firstly, we will be continuing testing. As
I said at the start, we are still relatively

00:54:33.280 --> 00:54:38.560
in the early phases of some of the testing,
so we have more to do. Secondly, we have committed

00:54:38.560 --> 00:54:44.300
to evaluation, so we are evaluating in a number
of ways, and some of our patient groups have

00:54:44.300 --> 00:54:49.920
very kindly stepped up to undertake a valuation
in terms of patient experience, and I’m

00:54:49.920 --> 00:54:54.260
very grateful to Healthwatch and others. And
then we are commissioning academic partners,

00:54:54.260 --> 00:55:01.740
or an academic partner, for some of the evaluation,
as well. We also wish to consult. The point

00:55:01.740 --> 00:55:07.119
at which we do is contingent, of course, on
the testing and a number of other things,

00:55:07.119 --> 00:55:13.180
but that is something that we committed to
do, and we remain committed to do.

00:55:13.180 --> 00:55:18.599
So, I think I will pause there to take questions.
Pauline, do you want to add anything additional

00:55:18.599 --> 00:55:24.150
at high level on the urgent and emergency
care, because I know that’s something that

00:55:24.150 --> 00:55:27.670
you’re very involved in.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: I think, Steve, just to

00:55:27.670 --> 00:55:33.730
reinforce what you’ve said – and, you
know, I think by working with the 14 departments,

00:55:33.730 --> 00:55:38.690
there is a real atmosphere in those departments
that every minute counts for every patient,

00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:44.069
and I think that’s why we’ve had a significant
level of clinical interest in this. And if

00:55:44.069 --> 00:55:49.550
by counting other metrics, will it support
our transformation journey better than the

00:55:49.550 --> 00:55:56.200
metrics we have at the moment, clearly, it’s
still very early days, and we need for those

00:55:56.200 --> 00:56:02.290
departments to collect more data. At the moment,
the focus has been on the mean, but also,

00:56:02.290 --> 00:56:07.900
time to initial assessment, and now, increasingly,
on the critical conditions, the patients that

00:56:07.900 --> 00:56:12.760
we would expect to be seen in a very short
time. But I think an awful lot of cooperation

00:56:12.760 --> 00:56:16.010
from the 14 departments, so we’re really
pleased about that.

00:56:16.010 --> 00:56:18.290
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Joanne?

00:56:18.290 --> 00:56:24.190
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thank you, Steve. I think this
is tremendously interesting. And I guess the

00:56:24.190 --> 00:56:28.250
question I would ask is that, it sounds like
you’re taking this to a really good level

00:56:28.250 --> 00:56:33.920
of sophistication and testing it extremely
thoroughly. From the point of view of the

00:56:33.920 --> 00:56:41.079
kind of member of the public, the existing
standards – I mean, they may be crude, and

00:56:41.079 --> 00:56:49.109
they may be subject – susceptible to some
gaming, and they might be being imposed in

00:56:49.109 --> 00:56:53.890
some – in ways that some people might consider
a bit sort of crude and brutal. But they have

00:56:53.890 --> 00:56:57.579
a couple of real merits, don’t they? One
is that they are very easy to understand,

00:56:57.579 --> 00:57:01.790
and the second thing is that they have had
a dramatic effect on waiting – it is argued

00:57:01.790 --> 00:57:09.210
that they had a dramatic effect on waiting
time. So can you help us understand how – whether

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:14.510
you see those advantages as being somehow
maintained in the new system? What are the

00:57:14.510 --> 00:57:20.850
trade-offs between the sort of simple, brutal
effective, versus something which sounds as

00:57:20.850 --> 00:57:24.490
if it might be a little bit more technocratic
and a bit more difficult to kind of get one’s

00:57:24.490 --> 00:57:26.950
mind around?
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes. So of course, as I said

00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:31.650
at the start in the introduction, we have
existing standards and they have in many ways

00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:37.170
served us well in the years that we have used
them, but they have strengths and weaknesses.

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:41.700
It is not actually the case that they are
always as well understood as we think and

00:57:41.700 --> 00:57:48.200
there is often confusion over the four-hour
standard, for example. And a recent, I think,

00:57:48.200 --> 00:57:55.079
Times article was corrected in a letter from,
I think, a retired doctor, who pointed out

00:57:55.079 --> 00:58:02.109
the four-hour standard is from arrival through
to the completion of treatment rather than

00:58:02.109 --> 00:58:08.650
initial treatment. So we still see confusion
in the minds even of those August people who

00:58:08.650 --> 00:58:13.109
write in newspapers, over these standards,
so I don’t think it is universally the case

00:58:13.109 --> 00:58:16.990
that every standard is as well understood
as we might like to think.

00:58:16.990 --> 00:58:23.020
But nevertheless, what we are trying to do
is to introduce, as I say, a set of standards,

00:58:23.020 --> 00:58:30.020
or propose a set, that will move us on and
build on what we have already. And very much

00:58:30.020 --> 00:58:35.380
a set of standards that is understandable
to staff and patients is one of those criteria

00:58:35.380 --> 00:58:40.609
which is exactly why we have asked Healthwatch
and others to help us in assessing that. And

00:58:40.609 --> 00:58:45.150
Healthwatch have been use – very helpful
in not only helping us understand what patients

00:58:45.150 --> 00:58:49.850
currently think, but they will be assessing
what patients think as we start to develop

00:58:49.850 --> 00:58:54.559
new standards.
And I should say personally I have tried to

00:58:54.559 --> 00:58:59.930
very much approach this as a patient rather
than a doctor because I'm a patient as well

00:58:59.930 --> 00:59:05.900
as a doctor and I think in setting any set
of standards, it’s important that there

00:59:05.900 --> 00:59:11.869
is a narrative that is as simple as possible
for the patient but also for staff. And of

00:59:11.869 --> 00:59:16.430
course, those are not always the same, but
I think it is important to get the balance

00:59:16.430 --> 00:59:19.600
right.
So in cancer for instance where we are really

00:59:19.600 --> 00:59:26.530
moving towards early diagnosis because we
wish – we know that early diagnosis of cancer

00:59:26.530 --> 00:59:31.420
means that diagnosis is picked – the cancer
is picked up at an earlier stage and that

00:59:31.420 --> 00:59:36.270
means that treatment is more effective and
that outcomes are better. So moving to a standard

00:59:36.270 --> 00:59:43.569
that focuses on faster diagnosis in other
words says to the citizen of England that

00:59:43.569 --> 00:59:50.960
from the point of referral to an urgent cancer
service you will have a diagnosis within 28

00:59:50.960 --> 00:59:55.030
days, most of the time of which is that you
don’t have cancer, but you will get that

00:59:55.030 --> 01:00:01.680
diagnosis in 28 days begins to shift the emphasis
of what we do onto picking up cancer and diagnosing

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:09.020
quickly. So, there is merit not only in what
we wish these standards to support operationally

01:00:09.020 --> 01:00:13.359
but the message that they convey around what
our intent is.

01:00:13.359 --> 01:00:18.000
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Mine’s actually on the
– a little about the timescales of this.

01:00:18.000 --> 01:00:25.470
Because we’re field testing at the moment.
We’d said that we wouldn’t be looking

01:00:25.470 --> 01:00:35.002
to introduce new standards from April 2020.
We've got evaluation and then consultation

01:00:35.002 --> 01:00:40.510
and then approval to go through, and what
I guess I wanted to get my head around on

01:00:40.510 --> 01:00:48.849
this is – is when as a board do we start
to see the evaluation and to think then about

01:00:48.849 --> 01:00:54.490
how we consult the next steps on this? Because
they are so critical these standards.

01:00:54.490 --> 01:01:00.160
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes. So as I said, there are
a number – the various standards or the

01:01:00.160 --> 01:01:06.900
various testing is being approached on different
timelines. So, there are a number of things

01:01:06.900 --> 01:01:10.500
that we need to consider in the overall timeline.
One is the individual timeline, so mental

01:01:10.500 --> 01:01:16.470
health will take us longer because of the
deployment of resource into the system as

01:01:16.470 --> 01:01:21.090
part of the long-term plan and indeed the
five-year forward view in mental health, which

01:01:21.090 --> 01:01:27.250
will allow those standards to be introduced.
The outcome of testing: so obviously we want

01:01:27.250 --> 01:01:34.300
the testing to support recommendations and
introduction of standards. That’s what we

01:01:34.300 --> 01:01:39.240
choose to do, or choose to recommend, and
of course we’re still in that testing phase.

01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:46.730
There are a number of obviously key points
in the NHS rhythm of the year. 1st April is

01:01:46.730 --> 01:01:51.809
a very important point not just because it’s
our financial year: because it is the point

01:01:51.809 --> 01:01:59.140
at which the mandate for the year from the
government takes effect. So, we are working

01:01:59.140 --> 01:02:07.940
towards if everything goes as we would wish
possibly a 1st April implementation for urgent

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:14.980
and emergency care, but on the others and
very possibly for the cancer 28-day standard.

01:02:14.980 --> 01:02:18.800
But we will need to work and see where we
are over the next month or two in terms of

01:02:18.800 --> 01:02:26.150
the pilots. The consultation is also important
and we need to work that in and of course

01:02:26.150 --> 01:02:37.460
some of these, in particular on elective care
and on cancer, are in the NHS constitution,

01:02:37.460 --> 01:02:41.910
and we are working closely with the department
of health and social care over the approach

01:02:41.910 --> 01:02:46.859
to legislative changes, if they are required
in order to progress those.

01:02:46.859 --> 01:02:49.130
So a number of moving parts, but some indicative
thoughts in our minds.

01:02:49.130 --> 01:02:54.120
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I think you answered the
second part of the question I asked. Do these

01:02:54.120 --> 01:02:59.040
automatically then – if we have new standards
they automatically roll into the constitution

01:02:59.040 --> 01:03:02.290
as replacements for existing ones?
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: So it depends. I'm not going

01:03:02.290 --> 01:03:04.250
to give you a complex answer –
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: No, keep it simple.

01:03:04.250 --> 01:03:07.119
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: - because it’s a complex
answer, which is why I think I chose my words

01:03:07.119 --> 01:03:10.710
carefully and said potentially, because it
rather depends what we recommend and how we

01:03:10.710 --> 01:03:17.660
recommend it and the approach we’re taking.
But yes, it may require possibly secondary

01:03:17.660 --> 01:03:20.829
legislation, but that is still to be arrived
at.

01:03:20.829 --> 01:03:26.660
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Quite a lot of conditional....
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: I am absolutely conditioning

01:03:26.660 --> 01:03:32.270
that with a lot of conditions until we know
what we’re recommending, I can’t give

01:03:32.270 --> 01:03:35.251
you the answer.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: That is fair enough. Other

01:03:35.251 --> 01:03:43.000
questions or comments on this. So, this is
going to be coming back several times I expect,

01:03:43.000 --> 01:03:49.840
so good to have an update, and thank you to
everyone who’s working so hard out in the

01:03:49.840 --> 01:03:58.780
trusts testing and trialling this. Okay. Thank
you. So we move on. It flows nicely to current

01:03:58.780 --> 01:04:06.650
operational quality and financial performance.
I thought we might start on the sort of operational

01:04:06.650 --> 01:04:12.150
and quality side and just start by welcoming
Amanda and having you just give us your sort

01:04:12.150 --> 01:04:15.950
of first impressions, and then I'm sure you'll
hand over to Pauline and then Julian.

01:04:15.950 --> 01:04:20.119
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: I will. Thank you very
much. Thank you very much indeed. I think

01:04:20.119 --> 01:04:27.410
– I think we discussed this sort of at the
very outset of the meeting, that what we see

01:04:27.410 --> 01:04:33.200
in operational performance at the moment actually
is the NHS doing largely an extraordinary

01:04:33.200 --> 01:04:38.579
job despite really quite significant pressure.
So, I think it’s really – we are very

01:04:38.579 --> 01:04:44.069
aware of just quite how hard colleagues from
across the whole of the NHS are working every

01:04:44.069 --> 01:04:49.480
day to provide the best possible care for
patients, to do that as quickly as possible,

01:04:49.480 --> 01:04:55.670
and as efficiently as possible. And in the
paper, there are some real things to celebrate.

01:04:55.670 --> 01:05:01.070
So we've got not only the increase in activity,
the year on year improvements that are set

01:05:01.070 --> 01:05:06.160
out, but also I think crucially some of the
developments around the transformation agenda,

01:05:06.160 --> 01:05:11.140
so the new services that we’re introducing,
so we can see in the paper we talk about – Pauline

01:05:11.140 --> 01:05:16.630
will say more about this – increasing same
day emergency care, the acute frailty provision

01:05:16.630 --> 01:05:22.800
that’s being rolled out, the about to roll
out targeted health checks for patients who

01:05:22.800 --> 01:05:30.140
may have lung cancer, the HPV vaccination
for year 8 boys which is about to start, again,

01:05:30.140 --> 01:05:34.640
rollout. And I think the other thing that’s
just another thing to celebrate really is

01:05:34.640 --> 01:05:38.430
that year on year increase in mental health
funding which again we’re seeing translate

01:05:38.430 --> 01:05:45.609
into reduced waiting times for treatment.
So setting all of that as the context – and

01:05:45.609 --> 01:05:51.830
we talked about it earlier – I think there
is very real pressure in the system. We talked

01:05:51.830 --> 01:05:57.440
about pensions; I think it is worth probably
coming back to that because there's no doubt

01:05:57.440 --> 01:06:03.280
that that is having a material impact on capacity,
at exactly the same time as we’re seeing,

01:06:03.280 --> 01:06:09.550
of course, increasing demand particularly
around non-elective care. What can we do?

01:06:09.550 --> 01:06:15.970
I think David that was your specific question.
So I wont try and answer that comprehensively,

01:06:15.970 --> 01:06:21.780
but actually I think there is something important
about just recognising and thanking people

01:06:21.780 --> 01:06:27.540
for what they do now, and celebrating that
great stuff, because that is the everyday

01:06:27.540 --> 01:06:31.299
reality of what’s happening in the health
service. Not that everybody gets it right

01:06:31.299 --> 01:06:34.089
every single time, but the aspiration is absolutely
there.

01:06:34.089 --> 01:06:40.680
So I think I’d start with that. I think
the second thing is really focusing on then

01:06:40.680 --> 01:06:46.260
where are some of our biggest challenges.
And clearly, it’s linked partly to pensions

01:06:46.260 --> 01:06:51.630
but not exclusively to that. There is a question
about how we can best support the workforce

01:06:51.630 --> 01:06:56.410
in the NHS; how we can ensure that we are
doing everything the people plan sets out

01:06:56.410 --> 01:07:02.099
- the interim plan sets out around making
it – the NHS a great place to work, making

01:07:02.099 --> 01:07:06.520
sure that we’re retaining the talented and
committed staff we have. The investment in

01:07:06.520 --> 01:07:11.540
CPD I think will be a part of that, but that’s
clearly again not the whole answer. And then

01:07:11.540 --> 01:07:15.640
I think there is something for me about just
practically – third thing really. It is

01:07:15.640 --> 01:07:21.560
working with our regional colleagues, our
front line colleagues – and Pauline will

01:07:21.560 --> 01:07:27.570
say more about this, I think – which we’re
now doing to undertake a stock take, to make

01:07:27.570 --> 01:07:32.740
sure we are really clear about, I guess, where
our biggest pressure points are heading into

01:07:32.740 --> 01:07:37.960
winter and that we've then marshalled our
collective resources to give the best possible

01:07:37.960 --> 01:07:43.640
support to those organisations that need it
over these coming – over these coming months,

01:07:43.640 --> 01:07:48.680
alongside making sure, I guess, that we are
rolling out and supporting systems and organisations

01:07:48.680 --> 01:07:51.890
to implement what we already know works and
is effective.

01:07:51.890 --> 01:07:56.161
So I think those are just a few thoughts on
the kind of what can we – what can we do

01:07:56.161 --> 01:07:59.780
about it. But I know Pauline and other colleagues
will want to add.

01:07:59.780 --> 01:08:03.460
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding:. Thanks. Pauline?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip:. So yes. Just adding a little

01:08:03.460 --> 01:08:09.430
bit of detail as far as emergency care is
concerned and elective care. Starting off

01:08:09.430 --> 01:08:14.910
with emergency care, I think it’s set out
in the paper, our performance for the month

01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:20.569
of August was 86.3%, and clearly we would
like it to be higher than that. However I

01:08:20.569 --> 01:08:26.690
think the really important thing to say is
that for the month of August, within four

01:08:26.690 --> 01:08:33.689
hours, we treated an additional 37,000 patients.
So that really goes back to the point Amanda

01:08:33.689 --> 01:08:40.229
was making about how hard our staff are working
at a time when demand continues to increase.

01:08:40.229 --> 01:08:48.279
In August alone we saw demand growth of 3.9%.
Now, a small amount of that was down to how

01:08:48.279 --> 01:08:53.880
we have changed our services, but essentially
we are seeing an ongoing growth in demand

01:08:53.880 --> 01:09:00.369
month on month and the services have been
seeing this for a number of years. And against

01:09:00.369 --> 01:09:06.619
that picture as Amanda rightly highlighted,
we've got significant workforce issues. Some

01:09:06.619 --> 01:09:10.969
of which we were very much aware that we would
be dealing with, and that we've been working

01:09:10.969 --> 01:09:17.179
very hard to recruit staff, etc., but I think
in particular the pension issue is something

01:09:17.179 --> 01:09:22.409
that a year ago, sitting in this meeting,
we would not have been talking about.

01:09:22.409 --> 01:09:27.619
So I think at the front door of the emergency
departments, yes, our overall performance

01:09:27.619 --> 01:09:32.209
is not at the level that we would like to
be able to say. We’d like to be able to

01:09:32.209 --> 01:09:36.639
say it’s 95%, but I think the really strong
message is, our staff are seeing more patients

01:09:36.639 --> 01:09:42.059
within four hours, and working very, very
hard to do that.

01:09:42.059 --> 01:09:49.529
Just moving on a little bit for the picture
outside the very front of the department and

01:09:49.529 --> 01:09:56.469
looking at admissions to hospital. And I think
there you start to see the impact of our transformation

01:09:56.469 --> 01:10:01.610
work, and the reform agenda. And you know,
just drawing people’s attention to the fact

01:10:01.610 --> 01:10:08.239
that if you look at zero length of stay around
same day emergency care, year on year, an

01:10:08.239 --> 01:10:14.570
8% increase as far as the admissions are concerned.
Whereas for overnight admissions it was only

01:10:14.570 --> 01:10:19.739
2.1% and I think that’s testament to the
amount of work that’s been done to completely

01:10:19.739 --> 01:10:25.840
transform the model about how we are going
to care for patients in the future. Ultimately

01:10:25.840 --> 01:10:29.631
the idea being – and I think everybody in
this room would support it – that if you

01:10:29.631 --> 01:10:34.739
don’t need to spend a night in hospital,
well, nobody wants to be in hospital. And

01:10:34.739 --> 01:10:39.709
we've got clinicians right across the country
working with us every day of the week to further

01:10:39.709 --> 01:10:47.070
develop those services. At the moment we've
got 100 diagnostic pathways agreed, so there's

01:10:47.070 --> 01:10:55.919
100 different types of ambulatory care pathways
being used right across all of the NHS.

01:10:55.919 --> 01:10:59.570
Moving from same-day emergency care, just
drawing people’s attention to the ongoing

01:10:59.570 --> 01:11:06.550
work around the ambulance reform agenda, and
you know, when we look at performance of the

01:11:06.550 --> 01:11:15.050
ambulance service, they – the 999 service
itself has seen 141 more calls during the

01:11:15.050 --> 01:11:21.130
period of January to August. And when you
look at the performance against the categories

01:11:21.130 --> 01:11:28.820
– cat 1, cat 2 and cat 4 – higher, better
response times for all of those than when

01:11:28.820 --> 01:11:35.039
you compare them to last year.
If we move to 111 for the same period they

01:11:35.039 --> 01:11:43.199
have seen an additional – or they have experienced
– an additional 123,000 calls and the really

01:11:43.199 --> 01:11:48.959
interesting stat which I think very much links
to the ambition around the reform agenda,

01:11:48.959 --> 01:11:55.869
is that we have now reached a stage when you
look at August information where we are having

01:11:55.869 --> 01:12:03.619
twice as many 111 calls as 999 calls. So the
whole landscape is changing, and changing

01:12:03.619 --> 01:12:09.010
significantly.
Linked to that, 111 online – now 100% coverage

01:12:09.010 --> 01:12:15.280
across the country, and when we look at the
type of coverage it’s at the phase III phase,

01:12:15.280 --> 01:12:21.289
which means that a patient can receive a clinical
call-back. And looking at the conversion rate

01:12:21.289 --> 01:12:26.531
of people who actually go online to actually
ending up in an emergency department, that

01:12:26.531 --> 01:12:33.419
being less than 10%. So these services being
really the forefront of what we will be doing

01:12:33.419 --> 01:12:40.639
now and in the years to come. Behind that
a whole range of services being further developed,

01:12:40.639 --> 01:12:44.809
on a day to day basis. For example, urgent
treatment centres. And I think we talked about

01:12:44.809 --> 01:12:51.179
that at the previous meeting.
So that’s a bit of an insight into where

01:12:51.179 --> 01:12:56.369
we are with performance, but I think going
back to the point that was made earlier about

01:12:56.369 --> 01:13:01.489
what can we do to do even better, which clearly
is where we want to be, I think it’s the

01:13:01.489 --> 01:13:07.969
ongoing drive of the transformation agenda.
We are developing greater and greater confidence

01:13:07.969 --> 01:13:13.849
in that, and I think it is very satisfying
for the people who are experienced in delivering

01:13:13.849 --> 01:13:20.289
care within those very different services
than existed five, ten years ago. But also

01:13:20.289 --> 01:13:26.760
we've obviously identified a number of priorities
that we want to focus on, now and in the coming

01:13:26.760 --> 01:13:32.179
months, to better prepare us for winter. Again,
the board won’t be very surprised about

01:13:32.179 --> 01:13:35.909
what they are, because they’re things that
we've been talking about for a long time,

01:13:35.909 --> 01:13:42.309
but services like reducing length of stay,
better for patients who are in hospital and

01:13:42.309 --> 01:13:49.099
need to go home, but also freeing up capacity
for the winter period; GP streaming; focusing

01:13:49.099 --> 01:13:54.989
on weekend discharges; etc.
So if it’s okay, I’ll move maybe from

01:13:54.989 --> 01:14:03.300
emergency care straight to elective care.
And I think there, looking again at a similar

01:14:03.300 --> 01:14:10.420
theme emerging, whereby, whilst we may not
have hit the waiting list target from a percentage

01:14:10.420 --> 01:14:16.090
point of view, again, we’re seeing more
patients than ever before as far as OTT is

01:14:16.090 --> 01:14:23.679
concerned. If you look at the change in the
data from the month of June to July, you see

01:14:23.679 --> 01:14:29.419
the waiting list increase by 23,000. It’s
a small decrease but it’s significant that

01:14:29.419 --> 01:14:35.469
that is happening, and at the same time if
you look over the period that we are talking

01:14:35.469 --> 01:14:45.630
about, more patients being cared for through
our elective services. Sorry, elective care.

01:14:45.630 --> 01:14:54.199
Linked to that 52 weeks, we've talked about
the success that we had last year in reducing

01:14:54.199 --> 01:15:01.400
long waits, and we have now reached a point
where, if you compare the number of patients

01:15:01.400 --> 01:15:07.209
who are waiting more than 52 weeks in June
last year, to the number that are waiting

01:15:07.209 --> 01:15:14.030
now, we've been able to reduce that by 70.7%.
Clearly, still have some way to go: we want

01:15:14.030 --> 01:15:19.429
it to be zero. We’re working very, very
hard to achieve that. And again, just linking

01:15:19.429 --> 01:15:26.320
in the transformation agenda, working hard
to do things in a different way. The MSK service

01:15:26.320 --> 01:15:30.780
has been increasingly rolled out across the
country; we’d set ourselves an ambition

01:15:30.780 --> 01:15:34.800
for 15% by the end of the year. We believe
we’re going to be able to do better than

01:15:34.800 --> 01:15:39.689
that. And then other programmes as described
in the paper. You know, for example, the EyesWise

01:15:39.689 --> 01:15:43.909
programme, etc.
And then finally just to finish off with the

01:15:43.909 --> 01:15:51.639
point that Amanda made. We’re about to go
into a stocktake exercise similar to what

01:15:51.639 --> 01:15:58.059
we did last year. Working with our regions,
working with our front-line staff, our hospitals,

01:15:58.059 --> 01:16:05.110
our systems, to understand where they believe
delivery will be by the beginning of December

01:16:05.110 --> 01:16:09.809
and then by the end of March. And that’s
on a number of different metrics, right from

01:16:09.809 --> 01:16:15.539
ED performance through to cancer care, through
to the care of patients with learning disabilities,

01:16:15.539 --> 01:16:22.539
etc. why is that important? It’s important
so as we can support front line services to

01:16:22.539 --> 01:16:28.749
provide the maximum level of care possible
and to do it in a sensible way, understanding

01:16:28.749 --> 01:16:33.719
what work we can do during January, etc.,
etc. I think that’s it probably. Thank you

01:16:33.719 --> 01:16:37.179
everyone.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: If it’s okay I think we’ll

01:16:37.179 --> 01:16:41.960
stay on operational performance and take any
questions or comments on that before we move

01:16:41.960 --> 01:16:46.789
onto finance, assuming that there are some
questions and comments. David?

01:16:46.789 --> 01:16:52.239
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, Pauline, Amanda, first
of all, I think your core message which is

01:16:52.239 --> 01:16:59.570
that we have – we owe a huge debt of gratitude
to the people who are doing I think a phenomenal

01:16:59.570 --> 01:17:07.619
job in dealing with more patients and actually
as you said, 37,000 more people in A&amp;E. But

01:17:07.619 --> 01:17:12.970
it’s reflective – we talked a lot about
the supply side; essentially, what we are

01:17:12.970 --> 01:17:19.679
doing to try and boost supply to deal with
higher numbers. I am intrigued about how much

01:17:19.679 --> 01:17:25.380
we understand about the demand side. So, you
know, some of the numbers, you know, it doesn’t

01:17:25.380 --> 01:17:30.570
sound much in percentage terms but it’s
a lot of people. And it won’t be uniform

01:17:30.570 --> 01:17:34.639
across the country either I would hesitate
to guess.

01:17:34.639 --> 01:17:41.780
So I'm interested in, is it demography, and
therefore this is just going to continue?

01:17:41.780 --> 01:17:51.909
Is it change in behaviour – people are more
willing and will go to A&amp;E? Is it because

01:17:51.909 --> 01:17:55.559
they can’t get appointments elsewhere and
they think the only way – they're not aware

01:17:55.559 --> 01:18:01.570
of the full panoply of options and availability
that’s around? Or is it something else?

01:18:01.570 --> 01:18:06.099
Because I think if we understand those, then
it’ll obviously be a bit of all; but if

01:18:06.099 --> 01:18:10.340
we understand the drivers, we can start to
think about how we can help patients get to

01:18:10.340 --> 01:18:18.979
the best place for them and choose the best
place for them, as well as tackling the supply

01:18:18.979 --> 01:18:21.889
side. So I’m just intrigued as to what we
know.

01:18:21.889 --> 01:18:27.939
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: So I think the first thing
to say that we’re focusing on demand all

01:18:27.939 --> 01:18:33.189
the time, to understand right across the country
what is happening, why people are coming to

01:18:33.189 --> 01:18:37.130
the front door of an emergency department.
And clearly, sometimes they are coming to

01:18:37.130 --> 01:18:42.210
the front door of an emergency department
because we have moved services around. We've

01:18:42.210 --> 01:18:47.969
collocated Urgent Treatment centres, etc.
But I think if from the evidence available

01:18:47.969 --> 01:18:54.829
to date, we are quite clear that it’s not
just that: that we are having a genuine increase

01:18:54.829 --> 01:19:00.110
in demand, probably in line with what we have
seen previous years. But I think the really

01:19:00.110 --> 01:19:05.689
important point to make is that the service
is pretty full at this point. We have maxed

01:19:05.689 --> 01:19:10.949
out the capacity we have. We have maxed it
out from a workforce point of view, from an

01:19:10.949 --> 01:19:15.559
estate point of view, etc.
So if you keep seeing this level of demand,

01:19:15.559 --> 01:19:20.540
you eventually reach a point where services
can’t deliver the way they want to. And

01:19:20.540 --> 01:19:25.749
clearly that’s why the reform agenda is
very important to do things in a different

01:19:25.749 --> 01:19:28.409
way.
I think going on to why are people turning

01:19:28.409 --> 01:19:34.019
up, if we park the reform agenda to one side
– so they’re not the people that we are

01:19:34.019 --> 01:19:40.369
sending to the departments etc. – yes, there
is an element of the ‘here and now’ society.

01:19:40.369 --> 01:19:45.960
You know, if a patient turns up at an emergency
department, they know what that offer is going

01:19:45.960 --> 01:19:52.659
to be, and I think that the challenge for
us is to make that offer better away from

01:19:52.659 --> 01:19:58.130
the emergency department, and that people
should be able to turn up at our other services,

01:19:58.130 --> 01:20:03.130
have diagnostics at the time of need, not
need to return to it three times, so as they're

01:20:03.130 --> 01:20:09.239
not choosing to walk into the emergency department.
And I think we heard it earlier on being described

01:20:09.239 --> 01:20:13.340
as the sort of ‘Amazon generation’, and
you know, I think we’ve all changed our

01:20:13.340 --> 01:20:18.639
behaviours as to what we expect and when we
expect to see it. Clearly the important thing

01:20:18.639 --> 01:20:24.149
for us also is to understand on an ongoing
basis whether there is any changes from a

01:20:24.149 --> 01:20:31.510
population health point of view. We know about
our aging society but we’re constantly monitoring

01:20:31.510 --> 01:20:36.849
to see if there are any other issues. You
know, talking to colleagues across the border

01:20:36.849 --> 01:20:39.921
in Scotland, they talked about some of the
real impact they're having around drug and

01:20:39.921 --> 01:20:43.449
alcohol – we’re watching all of those
things as well.

01:20:43.449 --> 01:20:50.309
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So I’d encourage us to
really try and understand in some depth the

01:20:50.309 --> 01:20:59.399
drivers of demand, because winter will come
around every year. And therefore, I think

01:20:59.399 --> 01:21:05.739
we are in – we have done an astonishingly
good job at coping with increasing demand,

01:21:05.739 --> 01:21:11.429
but there are only so many levers that you
can pull. And therefore I think if we can

01:21:11.429 --> 01:21:15.900
understand that, you know, we can – the
demand side, we can start to think about how

01:21:15.900 --> 01:21:21.920
we can intervene higher up the chain, to make
sure that we can – we've got the right services

01:21:21.920 --> 01:21:27.909
for people who can choose in a sensible way
the right one for them, and hopefully then

01:21:27.909 --> 01:21:31.159
we can serve them better. Which is obviously
what we’re trying to do.

01:21:31.159 --> 01:21:35.130
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: And just to reassure you,
we are doing this all the time. And one of

01:21:35.130 --> 01:21:39.519
the really interesting things that we’re
doing at the moment is in a number of emergency

01:21:39.519 --> 01:21:44.539
departments actually sitting down and talking
to people when they come through the front

01:21:44.539 --> 01:21:49.289
door, asking them how they have used our services
or how they may not have used our services,

01:21:49.289 --> 01:21:52.540
before they’ve chosen to walk in, so we
can feed all that back.

01:21:52.540 --> 01:21:54.650
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So maybe not for the board,
but I would love to see that. So maybe we

01:21:54.650 --> 01:21:58.829
could pick up outside, because I would be
really intrigued to understand those drivers.

01:21:58.829 --> 01:22:02.610
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Manir[?]?
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I would absolutely agree with David

01:22:02.610 --> 01:22:06.659
on understanding the drivers of demand, so
I was going to ask about that, but I'm going

01:22:06.659 --> 01:22:13.280
to change the question. I just wanted to now
understand about numbers and demand increase,

01:22:13.280 --> 01:22:20.219
year on year, and the trajectory of change.
Is that trajectory increasing or is it plateauing

01:22:20.219 --> 01:22:25.670
off? So at least that gives us some idea of
where we’re going to be in five years’

01:22:25.670 --> 01:22:30.530
time. And the second question is, surely this
just can’t be a UK problem. Do we have any

01:22:30.530 --> 01:22:35.329
international comparators? So that we can
understand what’s going on in other countries

01:22:35.329 --> 01:22:39.050
and how they’re dealing with it as well,
so that we can learn from each other?

01:22:39.050 --> 01:22:44.369
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes. So you know, if we
just reflect on some of the stats around attendance,

01:22:44.369 --> 01:22:48.630
or something, we can talk about admissions
as well if you wish. But if we look at the

01:22:48.630 --> 01:22:55.289
growth that has taken place over the last
ten years, around attendances to emergency

01:22:55.289 --> 01:22:59.329
departments, it’s about 2.4% when – this
is what we would expect to see. That’s what

01:22:59.329 --> 01:23:04.570
we have seen. That’s the trend. Last year
it was higher than that: it was 4.2%, and

01:23:04.570 --> 01:23:10.280
so far this year it’s 3.5%. Now, we do need
to subtract something from that around the

01:23:10.280 --> 01:23:14.620
reform agenda and the fact that we’re bringing
patients to emergency departments. But we

01:23:14.620 --> 01:23:19.539
are seeing consistent, if not increasing,
levels of growth as far as demand is concerned.

01:23:19.539 --> 01:23:23.110
And that’s all part of the picture of understanding
what that demand is.

01:23:23.110 --> 01:23:28.760
&gt;&gt;Speaker: And international comparators?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: I haven’t got anything

01:23:28.760 --> 01:23:36.780
with me, but I think we are very much – we’re
looking at stats that we've had access. Looking

01:23:36.780 --> 01:23:43.939
at Scotland, looking at Northern Ireland.
And that everybody is seeing – or appear

01:23:43.939 --> 01:23:48.599
to be seeing a very similar picture to ourselves,
in particular looking at what’s happened

01:23:48.599 --> 01:23:53.300
over the course of the winter, and in April
onwards. And we have access to some of that

01:23:53.300 --> 01:23:57.249
information which we can share with you. But
unfortunately, I haven’t got it with me

01:23:57.249 --> 01:24:00.479
at the moment.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: That is a very important point

01:24:00.479 --> 01:24:07.219
in terms of all the countries in the union
seeing the same underlying trend through the

01:24:07.219 --> 01:24:09.369
summer.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes, yes. And I think, you

01:24:09.369 --> 01:24:15.659
know, even when we look at performance, we
have got some themes in common, in particular

01:24:15.659 --> 01:24:20.079
going back to the earlier conversation about
the pension issue and our ability to meet

01:24:20.079 --> 01:24:21.579
that demand. But happy to share that with
you.

01:24:21.579 --> 01:24:24.260
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I think it’d be great. I've
seen it but I think it’d be great if we

01:24:24.260 --> 01:24:29.360
could share it with the whole board.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes. Yes.

01:24:29.360 --> 01:24:32.709
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Joanne. You were keen to come
in.

01:24:32.709 --> 01:24:33.760
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: This is obviously so important,
and when you think that a lot of what you

01:24:33.760 --> 01:24:37.170
do is about supporting local organisations
to do the best they can to manage the demand

01:24:37.170 --> 01:24:44.369
– but in the interests of also sort of focusing
on the things that only the centre can do,

01:24:44.369 --> 01:24:49.469
the stuff that’s very much in our responsibility,
it’s absolutely fantastic to see that 111

01:24:49.469 --> 01:24:55.600
online is now available nationwide. And the
fact that only 10% of people who use it end

01:24:55.600 --> 01:25:01.639
up being directed into emergency care is incredibly
reassuring. So I suppose I have two questions

01:25:01.639 --> 01:25:08.519
around that. One is, now that we understand
what sort of impact it has, should we be promoting

01:25:08.519 --> 01:25:14.949
it more actively, vigorously and publicly,
given that for so many people now, the first

01:25:14.949 --> 01:25:21.969
place to go with any sort of problem is online?
And the second question I have is that, in

01:25:21.969 --> 01:25:29.610
a world where every percentage point or in
fact every basis point of demand makes a difference,

01:25:29.610 --> 01:25:33.959
continuously refining those algorithms and
understanding the patient journey through

01:25:33.959 --> 01:25:41.510
the questions, and tweaking them and refining
them so that we are deploying them in the

01:25:41.510 --> 01:25:47.209
most effective way to give people the right
answer who we unnecessarily send into care

01:25:47.209 --> 01:25:51.100
– can you tell us a bit about how that is
being done?

01:25:51.100 --> 01:25:57.479
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yeah. The first question
was around the whole issue of 111 and how

01:25:57.479 --> 01:26:03.070
we communicate. And clearly, we have been
careful in recent years to make sure that

01:26:03.070 --> 01:26:08.480
each of the services are sufficiently robust
before we communicate. You will be pleased

01:26:08.480 --> 01:26:13.570
to know that part of our winter campaign this
year is now around 111, because we feel that

01:26:13.570 --> 01:26:19.110
the service has developed sufficient resilience
that we really want to draw people’s attention

01:26:19.110 --> 01:26:25.829
both to 111 and 111 online. So that will increasingly
happen, and you will start to see the media

01:26:25.829 --> 01:26:31.679
around that in the coming weeks and months.
Sorry, your second question – I didn’t

01:26:31.679 --> 01:26:34.229
quite get it.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: How – what are we doing to

01:26:34.229 --> 01:26:41.489
refine the algorithms within 111 online and
also with phone, but particularly online,

01:26:41.489 --> 01:26:48.019
so we learn from patient journey through each
question, to refine and learn and make them

01:26:48.019 --> 01:26:53.230
more sophisticated, and reduce the number
of people whom we – who take a journey through

01:26:53.230 --> 01:26:58.360
the algorithm and end up being referred into
face to face care who might otherwise have

01:26:58.360 --> 01:27:02.119
been dealt with differently?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yeah. So you may recall

01:27:02.119 --> 01:27:08.419
that as far as 111 itself is concerned and
111 online, that’s something that we've

01:27:08.419 --> 01:27:15.249
been very careful to do in the roll out, to
understand patient behaviour and how people

01:27:15.249 --> 01:27:20.679
are availing themselves of the service and
then what they are choosing to do thereafter.

01:27:20.679 --> 01:27:26.489
So for example, if we say to a patient, we
honestly believe the best thing is to go to

01:27:26.489 --> 01:27:31.099
the local pharmacy, but you turn up at the
hospital, etc., being able to capture that

01:27:31.099 --> 01:27:36.969
data. And you know through the emergency care
data set, etc., we are getting better and

01:27:36.969 --> 01:27:43.460
better information around population behaviour.
So there's a significant focus on that, and

01:27:43.460 --> 01:27:48.449
again, happy to report it back in greater
detail for 111 and 111 online.

01:27:48.449 --> 01:27:53.010
But that’s how we know sort of the conversion
rate and who’s turning up in the departments,

01:27:53.010 --> 01:27:57.329
etc., and then we can compare that to who
we advise to turn up, etc.

01:27:57.329 --> 01:28:00.269
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I wonder whether, Noel, you
would attempt to answer – well, those of

01:28:00.269 --> 01:28:05.469
us who have been involved in sort of digital
service businesses that are much less important

01:28:05.469 --> 01:28:10.420
and much less sophisticated than 111 recognise
the question you're asking, Joanna, the importance

01:28:10.420 --> 01:28:15.320
of continual optimisation. With your NHS Digital
hat on, could you maybe give us a bit more

01:28:15.320 --> 01:28:17.119
from that perspective?
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Sure. And there's an important

01:28:17.119 --> 01:28:25.189
link to the whole eChannel strategy and David’s
question of demand. So of our four major digital

01:28:25.189 --> 01:28:36.090
products, NHS online, the new NHS app, the
whole ePrescribing programme, and the eConsultation

01:28:36.090 --> 01:28:43.050
programme, the demand that we’re seeing
in all four new digital channels is exceeding

01:28:43.050 --> 01:28:50.219
our expectation which in one way is beginning
to show that the products we’re putting

01:28:50.219 --> 01:28:57.099
out there are well received and well tested.
But in the Amazon generation, the digital

01:28:57.099 --> 01:29:02.730
channels are generating new demand, and a
lot of that comes through in the triage process,

01:29:02.730 --> 01:29:08.880
the algorithms which drive the triage process,
and people checking more often about symptoms

01:29:08.880 --> 01:29:14.019
that they might have otherwise waited for
a physical consultation.

01:29:14.019 --> 01:29:22.659
So the benefits if you take them in the round
which hopefully come through in Pauline’s

01:29:22.659 --> 01:29:28.630
figures to some degree in being able to cope
with that steep curve of demand are also being

01:29:28.630 --> 01:29:34.099
seen in different ways. The benefits of patients
knowing more about their condition, knowing

01:29:34.099 --> 01:29:40.329
where to go, knowing how to access the right
solution, going to pharmacy – which we’re

01:29:40.329 --> 01:29:44.630
going to talk about the new pharmacy contract
which is a massive breakthrough in terms of

01:29:44.630 --> 01:29:54.289
activating a pharmacy channel as a new part
of the spectrum of offer – it is a demand-driven

01:29:54.289 --> 01:29:59.710
healthcare system that we have. And the more
we create new channels the more activity we

01:29:59.710 --> 01:30:04.139
get, but so we get new demand that might not
have otherwise been there.

01:30:04.139 --> 01:30:10.079
So it’s a helpful suite and there are more
roll outs in the NHS app with new functions

01:30:10.079 --> 01:30:14.439
coming on stream next year which will help
improve the offers progressively.

01:30:14.439 --> 01:30:22.610
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any other questions or comments
on the operations? I have one, having just

01:30:22.610 --> 01:30:27.619
talked a lot about demand, I’d just like
to swing back to supply. Direct this maybe

01:30:27.619 --> 01:30:33.269
to Ruth, just to talk a bit more about what
we plan to do – this is with my People Plan

01:30:33.269 --> 01:30:38.909
hat on – to upweight the work on retention.
Because clearly, the single most sort of powerful

01:30:38.909 --> 01:30:45.669
immediate lever we can pull to increase supply,
is to make the NHS a better place to work,

01:30:45.669 --> 01:30:49.689
so that more of our fantastic staff want to
stay with us for longer or work a bit longer

01:30:49.689 --> 01:30:53.460
with us as a result. And I just wondered if
you could talk a bit more about what we’re

01:30:53.460 --> 01:30:57.130
doing now and what more we could do in the
near term.

01:30:57.130 --> 01:31:04.010
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Absolutely. Thank you, Dido. So
the NHSI started the work on the retention

01:31:04.010 --> 01:31:10.699
and retention and collaborative using a quality
improvement methodology two years ago now.

01:31:10.699 --> 01:31:18.929
We started with a third of our community and
acute trusts and all of our vendor health

01:31:18.929 --> 01:31:22.900
trusts. And slowly by slowly, we've been working
with all of our trusts. And we've got some

01:31:22.900 --> 01:31:28.449
great people working on this: Professor Mark
Radford in my team has been leading it with

01:31:28.449 --> 01:31:36.760
a number of great individuals from all sorts
of backgrounds. HR backgrounds, clinical backgrounds,

01:31:36.760 --> 01:31:41.739
economic backgrounds. And they’ve been superb.
And what we've seen so far is that the trusts

01:31:41.739 --> 01:31:46.550
that have done the best are when they’ve
got the HR Director and the Director of Nursing

01:31:46.550 --> 01:31:53.159
working together and jointly owning it. Not
one or the other, and indeed not delegated

01:31:53.159 --> 01:31:58.610
to a deputy of a deputy of another deputy.
And I think what we've seen is the turnover

01:31:58.610 --> 01:32:05.849
rate has gone from 12.5% to 11.7% and in mental
health it’s even better than that. It’s

01:32:05.849 --> 01:32:14.469
9.6% to 8.6%. So it’s working. It’s working
really well, but it needs to be scaled up.

01:32:14.469 --> 01:32:20.590
And that’s a recommendation that I'm working
very well with the Chief People Officer, Prerana,

01:32:20.590 --> 01:32:25.199
and we’re just currently working out how
we can scale up, what would be the return

01:32:25.199 --> 01:32:31.209
on investment. But we have nine or so – nine
individuals working extremely hard. But actually,

01:32:31.209 --> 01:32:36.389
it’s the trust themselves that are doing
that work. They're the ones that are sharing

01:32:36.389 --> 01:32:41.840
the learning; they're the ones that are focusing
down on what are the key things they need

01:32:41.840 --> 01:32:47.149
to do. What we’re seeing as well are the
trusts going, actually – it depends where

01:32:47.149 --> 01:32:51.469
they are. It depends what challenges they’ve
got: whether they need to focus on those people

01:32:51.469 --> 01:32:56.989
that are newly recruited in – not just newly
qualified but newly recruited in, where they

01:32:56.989 --> 01:33:03.289
need more nurturing, where they need more
care and career management.

01:33:03.289 --> 01:33:09.769
We've also seen that how do we extend those
people’s careers in terms of timeframe.

01:33:09.769 --> 01:33:14.530
So those people that are the over-50 mark:
how do we help them to have fulfilling careers

01:33:14.530 --> 01:33:19.519
in all sorts of different ways, right until
the very last day that they want to be with

01:33:19.519 --> 01:33:22.639
us?
So stretching both ends is really important.

01:33:22.639 --> 01:33:28.090
CPD is going to be critical. CPD is what people
tell us will make a difference to how they

01:33:28.090 --> 01:33:36.229
can have a career in the NHS. And I'm hopeful
that will be there and I'm hopeful now that

01:33:36.229 --> 01:33:41.789
we’ll work with trust Directors of Nursing,
university suppliers, trusts themselves that

01:33:41.789 --> 01:33:46.110
will be able to provide some of this training
that we’ll be able to keep people. So yes,

01:33:46.110 --> 01:33:52.239
it’s working well. But there's much more
that we could do to industrialise it.

01:33:52.239 --> 01:34:00.969
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you. Can we move onto
a verbal update on the month four financials,

01:34:00.969 --> 01:34:05.309
Julian, I think…
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: Sure. So at the end of month

01:34:05.309 --> 01:34:12.199
four, I mean, we've now got multiplied[?]
data then we had[?] at the end of month four,

01:34:12.199 --> 01:34:18.619
the NHS commissioners and providers out there
who spend the vast majority of the money,

01:34:18.619 --> 01:34:29.889
were off plan by £75 million on a £39.5
billion spend. So sort of more or less holding

01:34:29.889 --> 01:34:35.539
it there, and forecasting broadly speaking
to be on plan. What’s interesting when you

01:34:35.539 --> 01:34:45.159
sort of dig under the numbers is that somewhere
around 25%, 49 commissioners are showing a

01:34:45.159 --> 01:34:55.619
sort of an adverse variance to plan and broadly
the other 75% are absolutely on track. And

01:34:55.619 --> 01:35:02.869
for providers, probably roughly a third are
actually showing an adverse variance to plan,

01:35:02.869 --> 01:35:09.399
although the variance per trust is much smaller
than that for commissioners, and the vast

01:35:09.399 --> 01:35:17.362
bulk are actually showing just to be slightly
ahead of plan. Probably looking at the forecast

01:35:17.362 --> 01:35:23.079
numbers now is not worth too much money. What
we’re actually really trying to assess is,

01:35:23.079 --> 01:35:28.760
what’s the risk that we’re really sitting
on? And I guess the great advantage this year

01:35:28.760 --> 01:35:31.080
of bringing you and I together is the ability
to –

01:35:31.080 --> 01:35:34.409
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: To see it in the round.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: To properly look at commissioners

01:35:34.409 --> 01:35:39.429
and providers in the round. Which I have to
say is probably – we’re also seeing real

01:35:39.429 --> 01:35:46.110
evidence of that and enabling much better
system conversations about how problems and

01:35:46.110 --> 01:35:52.749
financial problems and performance problems
together are being solved. But sat here today,

01:35:52.749 --> 01:35:58.699
we think we are sat on a kind of material
risk of sort of £5-600 million. Which in

01:35:58.699 --> 01:36:02.929
the scheme of about, you know, £120 billion
of spend might not sound a lot, but would

01:36:02.929 --> 01:36:11.790
be hugely problematic. And of course, different
this year to maybe last is that we really

01:36:11.790 --> 01:36:19.079
have put the money out into the system through
the increase in prices and allocations, and

01:36:19.079 --> 01:36:24.199
indeed then the additional support through
the provider sustainability fund and the financial

01:36:24.199 --> 01:36:31.399
recovery fund. So we absolutely do need the
system commissioners and providers to deliver

01:36:31.399 --> 01:36:38.639
against the plans that they have themselves
agreed through the planning process.

01:36:38.639 --> 01:36:44.340
So loads of work going on, largely through
our regional teams, with systems and then

01:36:44.340 --> 01:36:51.199
with individual organisations. I guess I sit
here and there are definitely some trusts

01:36:51.199 --> 01:36:55.190
we are bothered about. And probably the risk
we are looking at is split about equally between

01:36:55.190 --> 01:37:03.300
trust and commissioners. But probably this
year, compared to last, we are more materially

01:37:03.300 --> 01:37:10.389
worried about the commissioner position, and
we have seen certain groups of commissioners

01:37:10.389 --> 01:37:16.429
in the north west and in the midlands in particular,
showing material adverse – and so whilst

01:37:16.429 --> 01:37:22.719
across the whole country it’s how we’re
driving the improvement productivity programmes,

01:37:22.719 --> 01:37:28.380
on the commissioner side I think in particular,
how do we really drive better value, improved

01:37:28.380 --> 01:37:34.760
cost control, around prescribing – both
low value prescribing, the medicine reviews,

01:37:34.760 --> 01:37:44.129
the gearing up of GPs and pharmacists to really
get a grip of some of these issues with commissioners.

01:37:44.129 --> 01:37:48.809
And then we were having to think, at least
in a few places, is there a more structural

01:37:48.809 --> 01:37:53.989
intervention required, which is the sort of
ongoing conversation we are having with our

01:37:53.989 --> 01:38:01.119
regional directors at the moment.
That’s probably what is worth saying now.

01:38:01.119 --> 01:38:03.829
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: Actually, I will say, sorry,

01:38:03.829 --> 01:38:09.289
just two things. I think next time we come
back, we’ll have more to say on capital.

01:38:09.289 --> 01:38:14.260
Actually, more capital has got into the system,
but we have a real requirement, particularly

01:38:14.260 --> 01:38:21.570
from the provider sector, for taut capital
forecasts as we now are at the mid-year point.

01:38:21.570 --> 01:38:26.679
In large part, to work out, do we actually
have the capacity to release some more funding

01:38:26.679 --> 01:38:31.829
to begin to do catchup work in particular
around sort of critical maintenance backlogs.

01:38:31.829 --> 01:38:39.159
And we can only do that if we have taut forecasts.
So that’s the real plea to the whole of

01:38:39.159 --> 01:38:45.639
the – in particular the provider sector.
I was just thinking of the retention point.

01:38:45.639 --> 01:38:51.560
When you dig under the numbers, in particular
in the provider sector, basically we are running

01:38:51.560 --> 01:38:58.631
slightly over pace on pay, and under-pace
on sort of none-pay, year to date. And on

01:38:58.631 --> 01:39:03.570
pay, what’s particularly interesting is
the kind of agency number. It’s basically

01:39:03.570 --> 01:39:08.260
holding the same rate as last year. I think
people had planned that we would be seeing

01:39:08.260 --> 01:39:14.110
a decrease. I think it just goes to the point
of how do we improve not just recruitment,

01:39:14.110 --> 01:39:18.840
but retention, and how important that is if
we’re honestly going to get a grip of some

01:39:18.840 --> 01:39:25.840
of the cost drivers which are giving us problems.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes. Yes. Agreed. Any questions

01:39:25.840 --> 01:39:29.929
for Julian? Or comments.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Dido, can I make a comment?

01:39:29.929 --> 01:39:33.340
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Andrew.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: So much of our potential

01:39:33.340 --> 01:39:40.610
success going forward depends on system working
and getting a grip locally of the issues.

01:39:40.610 --> 01:39:48.409
How well do we feel that system working is
being developed? Because I think we’ve still

01:39:48.409 --> 01:39:56.059
got a range of approaches and you know, we
tend to look at the top-performing systems,

01:39:56.059 --> 01:40:01.739
but the big focus should be on the other end
of the scale really, about how we can support

01:40:01.739 --> 01:40:06.619
local leaders to actually get a grip on some
of the issues we've talked about, be it finance

01:40:06.619 --> 01:40:11.900
and performance – and I think you know,
anything we can do, and Amanda in particular,

01:40:11.900 --> 01:40:17.969
to encourage people to work together and actually
handle and tackle some of these issues going

01:40:17.969 --> 01:40:22.229
forward, will be our success or failure as
we get into the winter.

01:40:22.229 --> 01:40:24.729
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Amanda, do you want to pick
that up? I know you’ve spent quite a bit

01:40:24.729 --> 01:40:29.539
of time with system leaders in your first
few weeks in the job.

01:40:29.539 --> 01:40:38.329
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Happy to. So I think really
important point, and it’s clearly a both/and

01:40:38.329 --> 01:40:42.929
at the moment, because we are both working
through a kind of well-established and well

01:40:42.929 --> 01:40:46.891
understood set of arrangements based around
sort of individual organisations within the

01:40:46.891 --> 01:40:56.099
system, and we’re working with systems and
that’s got to be right. But I think the

01:40:56.099 --> 01:41:01.789
work that certainly we’re trying to do – executive
team, but particularly working with Ian, with

01:41:01.789 --> 01:41:09.340
Julian, with other colleagues, is now to try
and really hone down over the next couple

01:41:09.340 --> 01:41:15.469
of months, what the ask is of systems. And
we've literally just had a conversation about

01:41:15.469 --> 01:41:20.760
it before this meeting. And yesterday we’re
out talking to ICS Leeds, and Chairs about

01:41:20.760 --> 01:41:25.400
their experiences. At another event last week
with a different group of people, talking

01:41:25.400 --> 01:41:28.840
about, you know, their different perspectives
on different parts of the system. And I think

01:41:28.840 --> 01:41:37.400
what comes through is a strong desire for
greater clarity; an equally strong desire

01:41:37.400 --> 01:41:42.349
for that to be co-created with the NHs, which
I think we would all agree is absolutely right.

01:41:42.349 --> 01:41:51.169
And a sense of kind of getting the right balance
between recognising the very successful variation

01:41:51.169 --> 01:41:57.039
that exists at the moment which is good and
responsive to local needs, but providing sufficient

01:41:57.039 --> 01:42:03.300
clarity about direction of travel and about
expectations of systems, such that people

01:42:03.300 --> 01:42:07.530
are empowered to get on and do the stuff they
need to do. So I think that’s the bit that

01:42:07.530 --> 01:42:10.699
we’re just trying to crystallise and we’ll
be trying to do that over the next couple

01:42:10.699 --> 01:42:13.619
of months.
In parallel though, and I think kind of importantly

01:42:13.619 --> 01:42:18.919
because that could be a sort of theoretical
exercise, we've also agreed with regional

01:42:18.919 --> 01:42:26.249
colleagues some areas, now, that we will start
to work in practice to try and sort of prototype

01:42:26.249 --> 01:42:34.300
what that kind of, I suppose, genuine support
model could look like at system level that

01:42:34.300 --> 01:42:37.670
would touch on some of the kind of financial
realities of what needs to be delivered but

01:42:37.670 --> 01:42:42.409
very much alongside a focus on all of the
other things we've talked about around quality

01:42:42.409 --> 01:42:45.280
and constitutional standards as well.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Thank you. That was really

01:42:45.280 --> 01:42:46.719
helpful.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: And I think that’s probably

01:42:46.719 --> 01:42:50.401
a very good summary of the whole discussion
which is that we need to be able to focus

01:42:50.401 --> 01:42:55.659
both on the long-term direction of travel,
and consult and engage and bring people with

01:42:55.659 --> 01:43:01.610
us, but also act in the here and now, recognising
quite how hard staff are working across the

01:43:01.610 --> 01:43:07.389
NHS, and how important it is that we support
them and deploy resources to support them

01:43:07.389 --> 01:43:12.860
in the areas that most need it as we run in
towards winter. Okay. Any final comments on

01:43:12.860 --> 01:43:15.010
operational and financial performance? Shall
we move on? Okay. Thank you all. So Ian: back

01:43:15.010 --> 01:43:19.179
to you. Community pharmacy.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much. Can I invite

01:43:19.179 --> 01:43:20.730
Ed to join us?
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Excellent.

01:43:20.730 --> 01:43:32.309
&gt;&gt; Ian Dodge: [Inaudible] on community pharmacy
it’s fantastic today to be talking about

01:43:32.309 --> 01:43:38.599
community pharmacy here at the boards. In
July this year we reached a landmark deal

01:43:38.599 --> 01:43:44.439
which marks a sea-change in our relationship
with the sector. And I want to pay tribute

01:43:44.439 --> 01:43:49.489
particularly to Simon Dukes, the Chief Executive
of the Pharmaceutical Services Negotiating

01:43:49.489 --> 01:43:53.689
Committee; Janette Howe in the Department
of Health and Social Care and her team, and

01:43:53.689 --> 01:43:59.149
Ed and Lisa here in NHS England and Improvement,
and their team, who have really brought an

01:43:59.149 --> 01:44:04.010
energy and a focus to our work on community
pharmacy.

01:44:04.010 --> 01:44:09.570
This is the first ever five-year settlement
in NHS history, and it provides really important

01:44:09.570 --> 01:44:15.439
financial certainty for pharmacies at a period
of profound change, because the traditional

01:44:15.439 --> 01:44:22.260
high street model is being interrupted by
the rapid growth of online dispensing. And

01:44:22.260 --> 01:44:28.419
to assist with reform of dispensing and help
confront some of those challenges this deal

01:44:28.419 --> 01:44:35.550
will now enable wider use of hub and spoke
automation for all pharmacies; small independents

01:44:35.550 --> 01:44:42.559
as well as the large multiples, as well as
changes to skill mix. Pharmacies will now

01:44:42.559 --> 01:44:51.429
under this deal do more for the NHS, delivering
new clinical services. They become a more

01:44:51.429 --> 01:44:59.219
integral part of the urgent care system, something,
Noel, Pauline, you’ve just been talking

01:44:59.219 --> 01:45:05.479
about. And this five-year deal isn’t just
for the medium term: it’s for now, because

01:45:05.479 --> 01:45:14.599
the new 111 referral service to community
pharmacy for consultations being booked directly

01:45:14.599 --> 01:45:21.090
starts this October in time to help with winter.
And pharmacies will also do more to prevent

01:45:21.090 --> 01:45:26.809
ill health. For example, by helping to detect
the very high levels we know of undetected

01:45:26.809 --> 01:45:32.829
hypertension which exist in our communities.
So this is, I think, a good deal for the sector.

01:45:32.829 --> 01:45:38.469
It’s fair for the taxpayer, and it’s beneficial
for the NHS, and I really commend it to the

01:45:38.469 --> 01:45:41.429
board.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you. Any questions or

01:45:41.429 --> 01:45:42.429
comments? Richard.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I mean, this looks very

01:45:42.429 --> 01:45:43.429
good. So I’ll start off with that.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Good start.

01:45:43.429 --> 01:45:49.170
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you for that Richard.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: There’s a but coming.

01:45:49.170 --> 01:45:58.449
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Because we have actually now agreed
it with the community [inaudible] it’s an

01:45:58.449 --> 01:46:02.300
interesting commentary but…
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: What we've got here, it

01:46:02.300 --> 01:46:08.519
would appear with the locals[?], is we've
got a deal with the pharmacies that will give

01:46:08.519 --> 01:46:11.969
us more from there. They’ve, yeah, coped
with – as the rest of the NHS already has

01:46:11.969 --> 01:46:19.239
coped with the increase in volume, and we’re
going to get that for about £200 million

01:46:19.239 --> 01:46:23.909
less in five years’ time than we paid two
years ago. So, eight year period, we’re

01:46:23.909 --> 01:46:30.459
going to pay them about £200 million less
and we’re given a shed load more stuff.

01:46:30.459 --> 01:46:37.379
Now that feels brilliant to me. So that’s
my bit about it. Brilliant. I guess my question

01:46:37.379 --> 01:46:42.469
is, how do I explain that? And the sort of
– there's one of three possible explanations.

01:46:42.469 --> 01:46:47.449
And you might say it’s the first one of
them. And the first one is: we did a pretty

01:46:47.449 --> 01:46:53.280
rubbish job in the past on this. Hand up,
I was responsible for this – for this area.

01:46:53.280 --> 01:46:56.599
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Ah, now we’re getting to
it.

01:46:56.599 --> 01:47:01.249
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Second possibility is,
yeah, here is a sector that really is transforming

01:47:01.249 --> 01:47:07.729
itself, that really is, has thought about
now how you do a lot more but actually spend

01:47:07.729 --> 01:47:12.449
less per unit of activity on it. Third bit
is, actually, we’re squeezing the margins

01:47:12.449 --> 01:47:18.679
too much on this, and we push them to the
point where that could create problems for

01:47:18.679 --> 01:47:22.270
us in the future. And I guess what I'm trying
to understand a bit is which of those three

01:47:22.270 --> 01:47:24.519
things?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I’ll offer a quick take and

01:47:24.519 --> 01:47:28.999
then Ed can come in and correct me when I
get it wrong. On your first point I’d say

01:47:28.999 --> 01:47:33.419
yes, community pharmacy has been, actually,
a bit of a neglected centre. And that’s

01:47:33.419 --> 01:47:38.209
I think both to the detriment of the NHS and
it’s also been to the detriment of community

01:47:38.209 --> 01:47:44.689
pharmacy itself. I’d say yes, it is transforming,
and there is huge scope for transformation.

01:47:44.689 --> 01:47:52.249
I mentioned dispensing reform. We've got a
– we spend a lot of money on a network of

01:47:52.249 --> 01:47:58.309
highly skilled professionals, primarily through
dispensing, and there's a big set of opportunities

01:47:58.309 --> 01:48:04.860
I mentioned. Hub and spoke automation I mentioned;
online distribution rather than just physical

01:48:04.860 --> 01:48:11.699
and face to face; and skill mix changes as
we end up with more pharmacists increasingly

01:48:11.699 --> 01:48:18.530
working in primary care networks, doing increasingly
complex work, not least bearing in mind we

01:48:18.530 --> 01:48:25.320
can’t get as many GPs as we would like.
So secondly yes, there is a really big efficiency

01:48:25.320 --> 01:48:32.949
opportunity. And then third in terms of the
value, this deal does provide stability. It

01:48:32.949 --> 01:48:40.939
comes against a context where there have been
recent cash reductions. We have also seen

01:48:40.939 --> 01:48:47.129
an increase in recent years in the number
of community pharmacies in this country, which

01:48:47.129 --> 01:48:51.889
we haven’t seen in some of the other countries,
and that follows market liberalisation that

01:48:51.889 --> 01:48:59.809
happened 15 or so years ago. And I would mention
that some of those dispensing reforms will

01:48:59.809 --> 01:49:05.479
help the sector deal with efficiency. And
one of the nice things about having a five-year

01:49:05.479 --> 01:49:10.099
deal is that actually it becomes in the interest
of the sector to try and work out how it can

01:49:10.099 --> 01:49:14.090
deliver some of the cost efficiencies now.
And one of the reasons why we struggle to

01:49:14.090 --> 01:49:18.900
unlock those changes is the extent to which
if you're just doing a one-year deal, it’s

01:49:18.900 --> 01:49:23.190
been less of an obvious reason for pursuing
that.

01:49:23.190 --> 01:49:27.699
And then I’d say what we’re currently
doing is rebalancing off spend. So community

01:49:27.699 --> 01:49:34.099
pharmacy can see a really important future,
more closely linked to the NHS, where the

01:49:34.099 --> 01:49:40.770
balance of spending between dispensing relatively
speaking goes down, bearing in mind the efficiencies,

01:49:40.770 --> 01:49:46.639
and we begin to work up more clearly spend
on sets of clinical services that properly

01:49:46.639 --> 01:49:50.639
connect with and support the NHS and indeed
the wider public health work we’re doing.

01:49:50.639 --> 01:49:51.639
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Excellent. Steve, you wanted
to come in?

01:49:51.639 --> 01:49:54.860
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yeah, well Ian made a very
good point, which was the focus on the pharmacy

01:49:54.860 --> 01:50:02.969
workforce rather than the community pharmacies.
And the pharmacists are of course an incredibly

01:50:02.969 --> 01:50:08.599
skilled workforce and it’s really essential
both for their professional satisfaction and

01:50:08.599 --> 01:50:13.860
for the NHS and for patients that we – that
we use them and all the skills that they have.

01:50:13.860 --> 01:50:19.519
So I think it’s important to see this in
the context of the increasing value and work

01:50:19.519 --> 01:50:26.239
they do in secondary care, to assist our secondary
care clinicians; in primary care, which is

01:50:26.239 --> 01:50:31.399
one of the cornerstones of the GP contract,
and of course they're not alone there, we

01:50:31.399 --> 01:50:36.169
include paramedics and others. But pharmacies
– pharmacists have much more they can bring

01:50:36.169 --> 01:50:41.439
to primary care. We’re supporting that and
GPs are supporting that. And then of course

01:50:41.439 --> 01:50:45.189
in community pharmacies.
So I think it’s a great example of how when

01:50:45.189 --> 01:50:50.760
it comes to the people plan we are ensuring
that we use the skills mix and all the great

01:50:50.760 --> 01:50:56.539
staff that we have in the NHS and other areas
that support the NHS to its fullest.

01:50:56.539 --> 01:51:00.980
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Can I add one other thing on
the workforce which is, apart from clinical

01:51:00.980 --> 01:51:07.590
pharmacists, we also have a huge opportunity
with recent reform and recognition and support

01:51:07.590 --> 01:51:12.139
for pharmacy technicians. And I think we will
start to see a real growth in their numbers

01:51:12.139 --> 01:51:19.219
in the next few years and that will help a
variety of sectors. It will help the community

01:51:19.219 --> 01:51:22.249
pharmacy sector and it will also help primary
care.

01:51:22.249 --> 01:51:25.969
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Brilliant. Mindful of everyone’s
time – Manir. Briefly.

01:51:25.969 --> 01:51:31.879
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Brief question. Good step forward.
Just wondering about the training requirements

01:51:31.879 --> 01:51:37.039
if they’re going to look after minor illnesses
with urgent care, and the training requirements

01:51:37.039 --> 01:51:40.919
might be different for somebody who’s working
in a single isolated pharmacy compared to

01:51:40.919 --> 01:51:47.969
those who work in a chain pharmacy and the
standardisation of that training.

01:51:47.969 --> 01:51:55.159
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So the way the service has been
designed for its roll out in October is that

01:51:55.159 --> 01:51:59.359
it effectively directs to pharmacy the sort
of conditions you'd expect to be walking off

01:51:59.359 --> 01:52:04.809
the street into a pharmacy anyway and in the
clinical scope of practice for a pharmacist

01:52:04.809 --> 01:52:11.309
in their training. So it may be more conservative
than in the long run with extra training and

01:52:11.309 --> 01:52:17.739
support you could support the workforce to
deliver. Nevertheless, we have to make sure

01:52:17.739 --> 01:52:23.780
that everyone delivering the service in the
pharmacy is competent to do so. So the service

01:52:23.780 --> 01:52:27.860
specification’s very clear about the obligations
on the owners of pharmacies, the contract

01:52:27.860 --> 01:52:33.379
holders, to make sure competent staff are
certified as competent and delivering in the

01:52:33.379 --> 01:52:37.340
pharmacy and we are going to make available
training courses to make sure that things

01:52:37.340 --> 01:52:41.749
like red flag symptoms for sepsis, etc., are
well recognised by the people delivering the

01:52:41.749 --> 01:52:44.130
service.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: So those training courses will

01:52:44.130 --> 01:52:47.150
be available online to the pharmacists so
they can access?

01:52:47.150 --> 01:52:52.639
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Those training courses will be
available in face to face, in proper training

01:52:52.639 --> 01:52:56.530
packages as well as online, yeah.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I think the point in the round

01:52:56.530 --> 01:53:01.719
which then goes into primary care as well
is ensuring that we support our staff in terms

01:53:01.719 --> 01:53:06.399
of the extra skills that they might need is
well made and in pharmacy in particular, the

01:53:06.399 --> 01:53:10.489
chief Pharmaceutical Officer if he was here
would make that point. And it is indeed something

01:53:10.489 --> 01:53:14.929
that we are considering as part of the people
plan as to how we address that.

01:53:14.929 --> 01:53:19.810
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: [Inaudible] John?
&gt;&gt;John: Mine’s just an observation, which

01:53:19.810 --> 01:53:24.709
is I had the great privilege to be out with
the Coop in Lincolnshire, who have 48 pharmacists

01:53:24.709 --> 01:53:29.559
– community pharmacies – just after this.
And I have to say there is – A, they do

01:53:29.559 --> 01:53:35.780
a phenomenally good job, being rooted in the
local communities; secondly they are really

01:53:35.780 --> 01:53:44.729
keen to play a full role in the local health
service; and thirdly, the length of the contract

01:53:44.729 --> 01:53:48.280
gives them some planning horizons which is
critical for them to think.

01:53:48.280 --> 01:53:53.539
So I think you know, they feel – and Ed,
I need to put you in touch with them. They

01:53:53.539 --> 01:53:59.780
feel really well placed, I think. The question
is how to leverage those sort of groups. We

01:53:59.780 --> 01:54:03.530
always think of the very big and the very
small: actually, there's a strong thing in

01:54:03.530 --> 01:54:08.379
the middle who potentially have huge leverage
for us. And one of the things that I would

01:54:08.379 --> 01:54:14.379
observe quite strongly is, the development
of the workforce we've got is, how do we link

01:54:14.379 --> 01:54:20.039
the pharmacists in GPs, the community pharmacists,
and the pharmacists in secondary to be able

01:54:20.039 --> 01:54:26.539
to give career development, career support,
etc., etc. Because actually I think it would

01:54:26.539 --> 01:54:32.050
be quite [inaudible] in a GP, on their own.
So I think we need to think about that as

01:54:32.050 --> 01:54:37.479
well as through the – I think we’re pushed
for time so I’d suggest you pick that up

01:54:37.479 --> 01:54:39.340
later but I think there's a real opportunity
there.

01:54:39.340 --> 01:54:46.550
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Take the challenge, and work
it, and move on. That’s okay. Excellent.

01:54:46.550 --> 01:54:50.409
Ruth.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: It’s been commented [inaudible]

01:54:50.409 --> 01:54:56.809
for information. My paper, Neil and Olivia
lead this piece of work. It’s an annual

01:54:56.809 --> 01:55:01.669
update of our public and participation work.
More things that we need to be doing, and

01:55:01.669 --> 01:55:06.479
we were having a discussion pre-board, but
it is for your information as a board.

01:55:06.479 --> 01:55:10.989
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Excellent. Are there any questions?
Clearly this is a very important part of our

01:55:10.989 --> 01:55:20.649
responsibilities in both boards. Okay. We
will take it as read if that is okay. Which

01:55:20.649 --> 01:55:23.979
takes us back to Ian again.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much. I’ll be

01:55:23.979 --> 01:55:30.349
very brief, to note that the government has
given a positive assessment of performance

01:55:30.349 --> 01:55:42.449
against the NHS England mandate. 45 of 60
assessed objectives being green or amber/green.

01:55:42.449 --> 01:55:51.289
Secondly, we now have a clear new mandate
for 2019/20 which covers both NHS England

01:55:51.289 --> 01:55:55.699
and NHS Improvement. And thirdly, henceforth
we will have a single integrated assessment

01:55:55.699 --> 01:56:02.349
against that, including formal six-monthly
reporting following the model we currently

01:56:02.349 --> 01:56:07.309
adopt for NHS England and we expect there
to be public assessment against that joint

01:56:07.309 --> 01:56:16.590
mandate.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any questions or comments

01:56:16.590 --> 01:56:23.619
on that? Good. In which case, is there any
other business for anyone? No?

01:56:23.619 --> 01:56:28.480
&gt;&gt;Speaker: CIL report?
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: CIL report is starred and

01:56:28.480 --> 01:56:32.289
that’s done. I've purposefully tried to
leave us a little bit of time at the end,

01:56:32.289 --> 01:56:36.349
because I am mindful that a number of members
of the public – and you’ve been very patient,

01:56:36.349 --> 01:56:42.239
sitting for a long time. I wanted to just
explain why we weren’t taking a question

01:56:42.239 --> 01:56:46.869
in the middle of the board meeting, but also
make sure that there is a bit of time for

01:56:46.869 --> 01:56:50.229
us to have an informal conversation with anyone
who does – who’s travelled to come to

01:56:50.229 --> 01:56:55.030
the board meeting who does want to. So just
so – and I probably should have said this

01:56:55.030 --> 01:57:01.669
at the beginning. Just so everyone is aware,
this is a – it’s two board meetings in

01:57:01.669 --> 01:57:08.349
common, in public; it is not a public meeting,
which is why we don’t take questions in

01:57:08.349 --> 01:57:14.239
the board meeting itself. And that is part
of the setup and constitutional arrangements

01:57:14.239 --> 01:57:20.909
for NHS England, that we've adopted as we
meet in common. But I think both Davids and

01:57:20.909 --> 01:57:25.919
I feel quite strongly that we do really want
to make sure that we’re accessible to members

01:57:25.919 --> 01:57:31.669
of the public; that we take questions wherever
we can both face to face and electronically.

01:57:31.669 --> 01:57:37.010
So I am hoping we have left ourselves an extra
15 or 20 minutes because the private meeting

01:57:37.010 --> 01:57:41.570
doesn’t need to start until five. So if
there is anyone who has been patient and stayed

01:57:41.570 --> 01:57:47.409
with us and is now in the rain who wants to
have some informal time with either David

01:57:47.409 --> 01:57:51.469
or I, we can free that time up now for the
next 15 minutes and we will do that going

01:57:51.469 --> 01:57:56.519
forward in each of our meetings. And I would
say David, who spent quite a long time – I

01:57:56.519 --> 01:58:00.440
don’t know if you want to take two minutes
to describe the conversation you had with

01:58:00.440 --> 01:58:03.380
the people from Swindon?
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Yes, so I think first of

01:58:03.380 --> 01:58:10.959
all is, they’ve clearly had a pretty bad
experience both locally but also in the way

01:58:10.959 --> 01:58:15.389
that we've interacted with them. And I think
we need to ask ourselves two questions: one

01:58:15.389 --> 01:58:20.480
is what happened, but secondly, why was it
that they felt the only way that they could

01:58:20.480 --> 01:58:25.340
get heard was to actually travel from Swindon
to here. And I think we need to ask ourselves

01:58:25.340 --> 01:58:30.099
some questions on that.
I've committed to go back to the lady, Kate

01:58:30.099 --> 01:58:41.669
Lenehan, who was, I think, bringing the raising
of concerns, and I think Simon, it’s something

01:58:41.669 --> 01:58:46.810
that maybe you and I can speak after, because
I've put my name on the line to make sure

01:58:46.810 --> 01:58:49.490
they get an answer.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you for doing that on

01:58:49.490 --> 01:58:54.990
behalf of both boards. So the public board
is now closed: if I could ask all the directors

01:58:54.990 --> 01:58:59.719
to reconvene at 17.00 and David and I are
available if people want to talk to us. Okay.

01:58:59.719 --> 01:59:00.320
Thank you all.

